00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:33.990 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: OK.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.390 align:middle line:84%
So today we're going to,
I guess, kind of

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.640 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:41.512 align:middle line:90%


00:00:41.512 --> 00:00:45.886 align:middle line:90%
folks from [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:00:45.886 --> 00:00:48.990 align:middle line:84%
which is actually very
closely related.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.650 align:middle line:84%
These are folks that,
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] the

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:55.550 align:middle line:84%
Comparative Media Studies
Research Group, based in the

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.295 align:middle line:84%
old Media Lab building
right now.

00:00:58.295 --> 00:01:02.210 align:middle line:84%
And you work with the shared
education program.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.555 align:middle line:84%
For folks who're unaware of
this, MIT actually has a

00:01:05.555 --> 00:01:06.025 align:middle line:90%
teacher education program.

00:01:06.025 --> 00:01:09.804 align:middle line:84%
So if you want to be a certified
teacher to teach--

00:01:09.804 --> 00:01:12.516 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Middle school
math and science.

00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:14.930 align:middle line:90%
Secondary math and science.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.140 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Then you can take
classes and basically get your

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:17.680 align:middle line:84%
certification of
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] certification

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.720 align:middle line:90%
at the end of those classes.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.970 align:middle line:90%
But they also make games.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:25.609 align:middle line:90%


00:01:25.609 --> 00:01:30.740 align:middle line:84%
Games using portable devices
like iPhones and PocketPC

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:35.860 align:middle line:84%
games that run on Flash to go
and prototype stuff before.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:38.920 align:middle line:84%
And focusing a lot on
games for learning.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:39.770 align:middle line:84%
So the education
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.020 align:middle line:90%
the research group.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.996 align:middle line:84%
The Learning Games Network is
kind of like a spinoff company

00:01:43.996 --> 00:01:45.100 align:middle line:90%
of the [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:50.797 align:middle line:84%
It basically sort of works on
projects that will be too

00:01:50.797 --> 00:01:54.080 align:middle line:84%
expensive to do inside
the university.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.390 align:middle line:84%
Because the university is
really inefficient.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.926 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:01:56.926 --> 00:02:00.870 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you can start by
introducing yourself?

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:05.307 align:middle line:90%


00:02:05.307 --> 00:02:08.250 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm the latest
in the group, so OK.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.280 align:middle line:90%
I am Constantine.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.110 align:middle line:90%
I come from Austria.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:11.910 align:middle line:84%
I'm at the moment a visiting
researcher at

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.952 align:middle line:90%
the Education Arcade.

00:02:14.952 --> 00:02:20.230 align:middle line:84%
And so I'd say I come from an
educational background.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.140 align:middle line:84%
So I did my Ph.D on learning
from failure.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.890 align:middle line:90%


00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:29.400 align:middle line:84%
And I worked on theories that
explain why we use, why we

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.060 align:middle line:84%
learn through mistakes and why
most of them don't want to

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:32.980 align:middle line:90%
learn from failure.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.150 align:middle line:84%
But in games we think it's
really funny, or entertaining,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.205 align:middle line:90%
or challenging.

00:02:37.205 --> 00:02:42.560 align:middle line:84%
So I was really interested in
how games can be tools to

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.340 align:middle line:84%
understand learning and
also, is it possible--

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.770 align:middle line:84%
this is one of my research
questions-- is it possible

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:51.220 align:middle line:84%
that people may learn, through
playing games, how to cope

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:52.580 align:middle line:90%
with failure better?

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.256 align:middle line:84%
Like, being more open to
realizing that sometimes in

00:02:55.256 --> 00:02:59.760 align:middle line:84%
life we make failure and our
expectations are not true, or

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.850 align:middle line:90%
sometimes maybe wrong.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:06.370 align:middle line:84%
So I do research on the
theoretical level with that,

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:08.820 align:middle line:84%
and I do interviews with
players on learning

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.070 align:middle line:84%
biographies and on how learning
experiences through

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:14.684 align:middle line:84%
games were important
in their lives.

00:03:14.684 --> 00:03:20.230 align:middle line:84%
And in the summer I was happy to
work for Gambit and design

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.470 align:middle line:84%
a game in the summer
production.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.980 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you heard about
that anyway, or--

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:25.420 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.494 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So it's
just like 8 weeks.

00:03:26.494 --> 00:03:32.920 align:middle line:84%
I was on a team and my theory
was used as the basis to

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:37.610 align:middle line:84%
develop a learning game
called Afterland.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:42.230 align:middle line:84%
And of course it's
free online.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:45.050 align:middle line:84%
And I think our students did a
really good job on this game.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.560 align:middle line:90%
And it's a platformer where--

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.008 align:middle line:84%
I will not tell you what it's
about because then I would

00:03:52.008 --> 00:03:54.960 align:middle line:90%
ruin it, but there is a twist.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.480 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm going to do now in
the next year is, it looks

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.450 align:middle line:84%
like I will try for a few months
and do research on how

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.580 align:middle line:90%
the game is used by players.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.420 align:middle line:84%
How they learn in the game, how
they reflect on the game

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:09.870 align:middle line:84%
and I will work with
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:13.844 align:middle line:84%
Johnson and hopefully even
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] in that.

00:04:13.844 --> 00:04:16.670 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:19.025 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:20.490 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Hi,
I'm Dan Reilly.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:21.347 align:middle line:90%
I'm a CMS alum.

00:04:21.347 --> 00:04:24.780 align:middle line:84%
I did a masters in,
finished in 07.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.610 align:middle line:84%
Worked with Philip a little
bit here and there.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.444 align:middle line:84%
Mostly I worked with the
Education Arcade during my

00:04:31.444 --> 00:04:35.316 align:middle line:90%
time at CMS and with Scott.

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:38.740 align:middle line:84%
And we worked on a project
called Lunar the Labyrinth

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:40.920 align:middle line:90%
which you may have heard of.

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:44.338 align:middle line:84%
It teaches middle school math
and literacy skills,

00:04:44.338 --> 00:04:48.306 align:middle line:84%
particularly to the at-risk
population that I believe

00:04:48.306 --> 00:04:50.290 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:51.740 align:middle line:90%
It's free and online.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:52.235 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:04:52.235 --> 00:04:54.215 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Better
than after me.

00:04:54.215 --> 00:04:58.670 align:middle line:90%


00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:00.650 align:middle line:90%
Got to keep it interesting.

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.630 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Is there a twist?

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:04.115 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Is
there a twist?

00:05:04.115 --> 00:05:06.590 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Yes, well--

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.460 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] twist that
you originally wanted.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.710 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.900 align:middle line:90%


00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:13.300 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: There
was a twist in

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.550 align:middle line:90%
the development process.

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:17.330 align:middle line:90%


00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:23.412 align:middle line:84%
So, that one was feted by the
Department of Education.

00:05:23.412 --> 00:05:27.645 align:middle line:84%
And it was a larger scale
project, but the grant didn't

00:05:27.645 --> 00:05:30.615 align:middle line:84%
go to MIT, it went to Maryland
Public Television.

00:05:30.615 --> 00:05:35.565 align:middle line:84%
So that was part of the, I
believe, impetus, for the

00:05:35.565 --> 00:05:39.975 align:middle line:84%
spinoff of the Learning Games
Network, is to house a

00:05:39.975 --> 00:05:45.990 align:middle line:84%
structure that can accomodate
the sorts of larger scale

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:50.380 align:middle line:84%
projects and think about them
in more sustainable ways.

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:54.020 align:middle line:84%
I'll let Scott talk
about that.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:59.750 align:middle line:90%
So, how did I get into this?

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:06.470 align:middle line:84%
My interest in learning games
is mostly around engagement.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:11.380 align:middle line:84%
I feel like people who are
extremely motivated and

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:14.200 align:middle line:84%
enthralled with a particular
subject will find a

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.100 align:middle line:90%
way to learn it.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.440 align:middle line:84%
And that a game may or may not
be the best way to learn

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.450 align:middle line:90%
anything in particular.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:26.200 align:middle line:84%
But that there are many people
out there who struggle to get

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.500 align:middle line:84%
interested in a topic
they're even trying.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:31.370 align:middle line:84%
And so a game can
be useful there.

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:37.680 align:middle line:84%
So that's an exploratory tool
to say, well, I never really

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:42.050 align:middle line:84%
thought about history, but I
like this game and I want to

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:44.125 align:middle line:84%
spend some time with it and I
want to be good at it, and

00:06:44.125 --> 00:06:48.224 align:middle line:84%
it's social, so now I'm finding
myself asking the

00:06:48.224 --> 00:06:50.970 align:middle line:84%
kinds of questions that other
people are telling me

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:51.960 align:middle line:90%
historians ask.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.154 align:middle line:84%
And I don't know what label they
want to put on it, but I

00:06:56.154 --> 00:06:59.430 align:middle line:84%
think it's interesting, and
just sort of having that

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:01.780 align:middle line:90%
introduction to a topic.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:06.900 align:middle line:84%
Also I think games
are helpful for--

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.900 align:middle line:84%
I guess I'd call it identity
exploration.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:14.190 align:middle line:84%
So for people, or particular
learners, who may not think of

00:07:14.190 --> 00:07:18.670 align:middle line:84%
themselves as learners or as
confident learners, that they

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:22.780 align:middle line:84%
can put that enemy behind them
when they enter the game

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.590 align:middle line:90%
because, it's a game.

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:25.960 align:middle line:90%
And anyone can play it.

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.610 align:middle line:84%
Or, maybe I'm particularly
good at games,

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.380 align:middle line:90%
so I can play games.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:35.023 align:middle line:84%
And then I can suddenly realize
that I might be good

00:07:35.023 --> 00:07:37.130 align:middle line:84%
at other things as well, or I
might be interested in other

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:41.510 align:middle line:84%
things that I wouldn't allow
myself to discover whether or

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:45.229 align:middle line:84%
not I was interested,
because it was a

00:07:45.229 --> 00:07:48.211 align:middle line:90%
risky sphere to enter.

00:07:48.211 --> 00:07:49.760 align:middle line:90%
So, current projects.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.840 align:middle line:84%
We're doing a language
learning game.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:56.320 align:middle line:84%
It's attached to a massively
multiplayer online game, with

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.596 align:middle line:84%
a grant targeting a population
of Spanish-speaking high

00:08:00.596 --> 00:08:04.185 align:middle line:90%
school students in the US.

00:08:04.185 --> 00:08:06.940 align:middle line:84%
And that grant is from the
Hewlett Foundation.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:10.796 align:middle line:84%
And then we have a couple of
other projects going as well.

00:08:10.796 --> 00:08:14.170 align:middle line:84%
We've got science and
some other stuff.

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.110 align:middle line:90%
That's about it.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:17.230 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So I'm
Scott Osterweil.

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.794 align:middle line:84%
I'm the research director
of the Education Arcade.

00:08:20.794 --> 00:08:25.150 align:middle line:84%
I have two titles, research
director and creative director

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:25.866 align:middle line:90%
of the Education Arcade.

00:08:25.866 --> 00:08:28.934 align:middle line:84%
But also one of the co-founders
of the Learning

00:08:28.934 --> 00:08:31.300 align:middle line:90%
Games Network.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:35.380 align:middle line:84%
And I don't want to repeat too
much of what's been said.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:39.270 align:middle line:84%
Except that at the same time as
I do this for a living, I

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.008 align:middle line:84%
still struggle with the
term educational game.

00:08:42.008 --> 00:08:44.175 align:middle line:84%
Because it's not the way I think
of what I do, primarily.

00:08:44.175 --> 00:08:46.050 align:middle line:84%
The first game I designed,
before

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.370 align:middle line:90%
working in this setting--

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:51.989 align:middle line:84%
the first game I did was
a commercial game.

00:08:51.989 --> 00:08:54.743 align:middle line:84%
It wasn't designed as an
educational game, it was

00:08:54.743 --> 00:08:56.120 align:middle line:84%
designed just as a puzzle
adventure game.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:57.350 align:middle line:84%
It happened to be about math and
logic, but that was just

00:08:57.350 --> 00:08:59.793 align:middle line:84%
because that's what we thought
was interesting.

00:08:59.793 --> 00:09:04.580 align:middle line:84%
It got marketed because of the
changes in the marketplace as

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:04.850 align:middle line:90%
edutainment.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:07.335 align:middle line:84%
But when I started in the
business there was no such

00:09:07.335 --> 00:09:08.790 align:middle line:90%
word as edutainment.

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:12.360 align:middle line:84%
Most games, if you go back to
the early 90s, were sort of

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:14.170 align:middle line:90%
exploratory environments.

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.400 align:middle line:84%
Either they were twitch games
or they were exploratory

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:18.870 align:middle line:90%
environments.

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:20.950 align:middle line:84%
And I have nothing against
twitch games, but the

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:21.260 align:middle line:84%
exploratory environments
were all about

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.160 align:middle line:90%
thinking and problem solving.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:24.455 align:middle line:84%
And to me that's what's
interesting.

00:09:24.455 --> 00:09:27.650 align:middle line:84%
No matter what domain you're
working in, whether it's

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:29.500 align:middle line:84%
mathematical or whether
it's historical, the

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:31.230 align:middle line:90%
processes are the same.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.130 align:middle line:84%
And one slight twist I want to
put on what Dan said, I think

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:39.046 align:middle line:84%
we're on the same page, but
because he led with the idea

00:09:39.046 --> 00:09:39.920 align:middle line:84%
of engagement, it's possible
to think that I'm talking

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.632 align:middle line:84%
about games as an inducement
to do something you

00:09:43.632 --> 00:09:43.990 align:middle line:90%
don't want to do.

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:46.880 align:middle line:84%
And I think that games are an
opportunity to discover how

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:48.299 align:middle line:90%
much fun something is.

00:09:48.299 --> 00:09:52.556 align:middle line:84%
Which is very different from
sort of, ah, we tricked you .

00:09:52.556 --> 00:09:56.886 align:middle line:84%
But rather, this is really
interesting, don't you like

00:09:56.886 --> 00:09:58.850 align:middle line:84%
doing this and you
can do more.

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:01.470 align:middle line:84%
And if that's all you can get
out of it, that's fine.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:05.234 align:middle line:84%
If you later realize, oh, I
like doing that and that's

00:10:05.234 --> 00:10:07.669 align:middle line:84%
math, and you do more of
that, that's awesome.

00:10:07.669 --> 00:10:08.643 align:middle line:90%
That's even better.

00:10:08.643 --> 00:10:11.018 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's not so much
because I just said it's fun,

00:10:11.018 --> 00:10:17.572 align:middle line:84%
it's because I think the very
process of thinking and

00:10:17.572 --> 00:10:18.822 align:middle line:90%
problem-solving [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:10:18.822 --> 00:10:21.420 align:middle line:90%


00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.940 align:middle line:84%
People do get that out
of games, and I

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.180 align:middle line:90%
just think it's important.

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:28.255 align:middle line:84%
It's how people realize, if you
enjoyed it in a game, you

00:10:28.255 --> 00:10:29.148 align:middle line:90%
can enjoy it in life.

00:10:29.148 --> 00:10:31.380 align:middle line:84%
And maybe this comes from
my own realization .

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.364 align:middle line:84%
But when I was in sixth grade,
instead of them saying the

00:10:33.364 --> 00:10:37.908 align:middle line:84%
scientific method, and writing
this list of dull steps on the

00:10:37.908 --> 00:10:41.366 align:middle line:84%
blackboard, they said,
play this puzzle.

00:10:41.366 --> 00:10:43.259 align:middle line:84%
Now let's talk about how
you solved the puzzle.

00:10:43.259 --> 00:10:44.330 align:middle line:90%
That's science.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:49.770 align:middle line:84%
I think that I might have
ended up working at MIT.

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:51.286 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, that's my spiel.

00:10:51.286 --> 00:10:53.920 align:middle line:90%


00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:58.034 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So talking a little
bit about identifying how

00:10:58.034 --> 00:11:04.965 align:middle line:84%
you're going to locate what's
enjoyable about the thing that

00:11:04.965 --> 00:11:07.875 align:middle line:84%
you want the people
to engage with.

00:11:07.875 --> 00:11:11.755 align:middle line:84%
Can you talk a little bit
about, maybe in a recent

00:11:11.755 --> 00:11:14.665 align:middle line:84%
project or in a past
project, how you

00:11:14.665 --> 00:11:16.560 align:middle line:90%
went about that process.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:20.362 align:middle line:84%
Let's talk about the things
that you worked out.

00:11:20.362 --> 00:11:24.306 align:middle line:84%
And maybe sometimes it was more
difficult and you had

00:11:24.306 --> 00:11:24.799 align:middle line:90%
challenges.

00:11:24.799 --> 00:11:30.715 align:middle line:84%
Maybe sometimes it was
just pretty obvious.

00:11:30.715 --> 00:11:32.687 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: I'll take it.

00:11:32.687 --> 00:11:33.180 align:middle line:90%
So--

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.138 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:11:36.138 --> 00:11:39.096 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I'd like to
congratulate Scott on his

00:11:39.096 --> 00:11:41.590 align:middle line:90%
personal growth.

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:45.774 align:middle line:84%
So, with the language learning
game that we're working on at

00:11:45.774 --> 00:11:48.470 align:middle line:84%
the moment, there are approaches
to language

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:52.727 align:middle line:84%
learning out there in the
world that emphasize

00:11:52.727 --> 00:11:57.920 align:middle line:84%
vocabulary memorization
or grammar.

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:05.410 align:middle line:84%
Grammar, meaning, memorizing
tables of conjugations and

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:07.905 align:middle line:84%
it's pretty much stripped
of any sort

00:12:07.905 --> 00:12:09.610 align:middle line:90%
of meaningful context.

00:12:09.610 --> 00:12:15.215 align:middle line:84%
So, and a meaningful context
could either be a text, and I

00:12:15.215 --> 00:12:18.898 align:middle line:84%
use that word loosely,
that is engaging.

00:12:18.898 --> 00:12:22.441 align:middle line:84%
It could be a film that you want
to see, that happens to

00:12:22.441 --> 00:12:25.280 align:middle line:84%
be in a language that you
are trying to learn.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:29.216 align:middle line:84%
Or it could be a means
of communication.

00:12:29.216 --> 00:12:31.696 align:middle line:84%
After all, that's what
language is.

00:12:31.696 --> 00:12:37.152 align:middle line:84%
So, if you're thinking about
using language as a means of

00:12:37.152 --> 00:12:39.136 align:middle line:84%
communication, then of course
you need to think about, well,

00:12:39.136 --> 00:12:40.128 align:middle line:90%
what are you communicating?

00:12:40.128 --> 00:12:42.942 align:middle line:84%
And, fortunately, games are
full of communication.

00:12:42.942 --> 00:12:45.158 align:middle line:84%
Between the game and
the player, and the

00:12:45.158 --> 00:12:46.096 align:middle line:90%
player and the game.

00:12:46.096 --> 00:12:48.675 align:middle line:84%
And if it's a multiplayer
game, between the

00:12:48.675 --> 00:12:49.450 align:middle line:90%
players in the game.

00:12:49.450 --> 00:12:51.630 align:middle line:90%
So it was almost--

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:54.070 align:middle line:84%
if you start from that
philosophy, it's almost a

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:57.402 align:middle line:84%
trivial exercise to say, OK,
well, what's something

00:12:57.402 --> 00:13:00.270 align:middle line:84%
interesting for people to
talk about in a game?

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:01.970 align:middle line:84%
And so you come up with
an interesting game.

00:13:01.970 --> 00:13:06.770 align:middle line:84%
And instead of having the
mechanic be, click this button

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:13.500 align:middle line:84%
to activate whatever the nuclear
missile, you have to,

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:14.530 align:middle line:90%
you build in collaboration.

00:13:14.530 --> 00:13:17.783 align:middle line:84%
So, I have part of the piece
and you have part of the

00:13:17.783 --> 00:13:21.185 align:middle line:84%
piece, and we need to talk about
when to use what and in

00:13:21.185 --> 00:13:23.897 align:middle line:90%
which context, and on whom.

00:13:23.897 --> 00:13:29.813 align:middle line:84%
And so, suddenly we're talking
and it doesn't even feel like

00:13:29.813 --> 00:13:32.771 align:middle line:84%
we're learning a language,
it just feels like

00:13:32.771 --> 00:13:34.743 align:middle line:90%
we're playing a game.

00:13:34.743 --> 00:13:38.391 align:middle line:84%
And then, if you're doing it
in a language that you're

00:13:38.391 --> 00:13:40.690 align:middle line:84%
attempting to learn, then
there's the barrier there

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:44.108 align:middle line:84%
where you might want to say
something that you just don't

00:13:44.108 --> 00:13:45.570 align:middle line:90%
have the skill yet to say.

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:49.318 align:middle line:84%
So it's not quite as simple as
just saying, hey, go talk.

00:13:49.318 --> 00:13:54.870 align:middle line:84%
But there's slightly innovative
ways that you can

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:58.839 align:middle line:84%
give people tools to allow
themselves to express

00:13:58.839 --> 00:14:01.740 align:middle line:84%
themselves in a fairly
constrained context.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:02.840 align:middle line:84%
So, because you've designed
the game, you

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:03.720 align:middle line:90%
sort of rig the system.

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:06.684 align:middle line:84%
You know that people aren't
going to talk

00:14:06.684 --> 00:14:07.854 align:middle line:90%
about absolutely anything.

00:14:07.854 --> 00:14:11.440 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they will, in the chat,
but primarily in the game

00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:13.520 align:middle line:84%
you're incentivizing their
discussion around

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:14.960 align:middle line:90%
a particular topic.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:19.280 align:middle line:84%
So if you give them tools to
discuss that particular topic,

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:21.670 align:middle line:84%
not only does it make the
gameplay more efficient, but

00:14:21.670 --> 00:14:24.406 align:middle line:84%
that's also how the learning
can happen.

00:14:24.406 --> 00:14:27.020 align:middle line:84%
Because there can be some sort
of selection mechanism,

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:31.512 align:middle line:84%
whereby I am able to say
something that I want to say,

00:14:31.512 --> 00:14:34.954 align:middle line:84%
but without needing to bring all
of the knowledge of how to

00:14:34.954 --> 00:14:35.810 align:middle line:90%
construct that on my own.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.327 align:middle line:84%
There's some amount of, oh, I
can just recognize that that's

00:14:38.327 --> 00:14:41.810 align:middle line:90%
more or less what I want.

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:44.300 align:middle line:84%
And then, by seeing it
repeatedly and by seeing other

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:48.687 align:middle line:84%
players use the same tools to
communicate, and knowing that

00:14:48.687 --> 00:14:52.655 align:middle line:84%
the communication in a certain
context is valid, that I can

00:14:52.655 --> 00:14:55.512 align:middle line:84%
pay close attention to that,
and start to become

00:14:55.512 --> 00:14:56.240 align:middle line:90%
familiar and learn.

00:14:56.240 --> 00:15:00.220 align:middle line:84%
But it's not, I have
to memorize it so

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:01.810 align:middle line:90%
that I can use it.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:06.088 align:middle line:84%
It's, I'm using it and I'm using
it so much that I just

00:15:06.088 --> 00:15:07.576 align:middle line:90%
can actually learn it.

00:15:07.576 --> 00:15:09.064 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Question on that.

00:15:09.064 --> 00:15:13.280 align:middle line:84%
So, if you're trying to
encourage me to talk Spanish

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:14.817 align:middle line:84%
and you talk Spanish and we're
trying to work something out,

00:15:14.817 --> 00:15:16.008 align:middle line:84%
what prevents you from just
typing something in English

00:15:16.008 --> 00:15:17.000 align:middle line:84%
and having you read
it in English?

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:18.883 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Right, so
it depends on the game.

00:15:18.883 --> 00:15:21.670 align:middle line:84%
And in some games that's
totally fine.

00:15:21.670 --> 00:15:25.700 align:middle line:84%
In other games we have teams
moderate other teams.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:29.280 align:middle line:84%
In other games, we have some
sort of communication tool

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:31.400 align:middle line:90%
that's not free text entry.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:37.910 align:middle line:84%
So it's not-- so one extreme
is, here's a list of 10

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:39.240 align:middle line:90%
sentences that you could say.

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.420 align:middle line:90%
Click one of them.

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:43.960 align:middle line:84%
And that's not the direction
we tend to go in.

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:47.390 align:middle line:84%
But that's an example of how
to communicate something

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:49.170 align:middle line:90%
without free text entry.

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:51.340 align:middle line:84%
So there could be some
sort of sentence

00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:53.450 align:middle line:90%
construction kit, let's say.

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:57.370 align:middle line:84%
Where you have a pool of
different words that you can

00:15:57.370 --> 00:16:02.160 align:middle line:84%
assemble and a variety of orders
to express a small set

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:04.460 align:middle line:90%
of meanings.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:07.080 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And if you think
about it, games like

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:12.220 align:middle line:84%
Charades or Pictionary, the
need to communicate with

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:14.112 align:middle line:84%
constraints doesn't stop
us from necessarily

00:16:14.112 --> 00:16:15.058 align:middle line:90%
enjoying the game.

00:16:15.058 --> 00:16:16.950 align:middle line:90%
It could make it interesting.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.870 align:middle line:90%
So, that's part of the game.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:23.740 align:middle line:84%
Part of our insight was
thinking about our own

00:16:23.740 --> 00:16:28.810 align:middle line:84%
experiences learning language
in terms of the game of say,

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.720 align:middle line:84%
and this gets at my core idea,
which is to find the game

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:33.301 align:middle line:90%
that's already there.

00:16:33.301 --> 00:16:36.035 align:middle line:84%
So, a game that I always play
when I'm traveling with

00:16:36.035 --> 00:16:40.168 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] when I travel
to France, where I speak the

00:16:40.168 --> 00:16:43.134 align:middle line:84%
language sort of, is how far can
I get into a conversation

00:16:43.134 --> 00:16:44.510 align:middle line:84%
before they figure
out I'm American.

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.460 align:middle line:90%
So that becomes a game.

00:16:46.460 --> 00:16:52.283 align:middle line:84%
And so the whole, and how can I
have or if I notice someone

00:16:52.283 --> 00:16:56.540 align:middle line:84%
who's French, how much
of a conversation

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:57.486 align:middle line:90%
can we have in French?

00:16:57.486 --> 00:16:58.432 align:middle line:90%
That's a game for me.

00:16:58.432 --> 00:16:59.380 align:middle line:84%
At that point it becomes
kind of fun.

00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:01.100 align:middle line:84%
So I think we were just really
trying to access that.

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:03.640 align:middle line:84%
And I think that really gets
back to your larger question.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:06.540 align:middle line:84%
When you take on something new,
I want to know where's

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.280 align:middle line:90%
the game already in this?

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:09.829 align:middle line:90%
And I [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:13.468 align:middle line:84%
the first game I think, it was
math, and the colleague with

00:17:13.468 --> 00:17:16.349 align:middle line:84%
whom I co-designed the game,
we both spent a lot of time

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:18.863 align:middle line:84%
saying, when we were kids, how
did we play with numbers, what

00:17:18.863 --> 00:17:20.181 align:middle line:84%
kind of things did
we do, what was

00:17:20.181 --> 00:17:21.139 align:middle line:90%
interesting us with numbers?

00:17:21.139 --> 00:17:24.500 align:middle line:84%
We really try to access
our own play.

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:31.540 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, now, if I can try
to read practitioners in

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:33.992 align:middle line:84%
the field and say, what do you
think about the shower, when

00:17:33.992 --> 00:17:37.278 align:middle line:84%
you run it-- where's the--
because that's where the play

00:17:37.278 --> 00:17:39.407 align:middle line:84%
is actually happening
in the field.

00:17:39.407 --> 00:17:43.040 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's where people
love their jobs, it's

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:44.880 align:middle line:84%
the play in it that they
love about it.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.993 align:middle line:84%
And that's what I think you're
trying to access in any

00:17:47.993 --> 00:17:48.712 align:middle line:90%
simulation.

00:17:48.712 --> 00:17:53.280 align:middle line:84%
So Dan, talking about the
language one, we're doing a

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.684 align:middle line:84%
game with the Royal Shakespeare
Company, if we can

00:17:56.684 --> 00:17:58.840 align:middle line:84%
get funding, around
The Tempest.

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:02.820 align:middle line:84%
And we're trying to figure out
how to make a game of it.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:06.890 align:middle line:84%
And I realized that part of the
change with Shakespeare

00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:11.434 align:middle line:84%
is, to some degree, the
language, too, and sort of

00:18:11.434 --> 00:18:12.980 align:middle line:84%
making sense out of the language
which is now somewhat

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:14.796 align:middle line:84%
archaic but really
interesting.

00:18:14.796 --> 00:18:21.020 align:middle line:84%
And so we tried to make a game
that's sort of a puzzle that

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:22.970 align:middle line:84%
you assemble by finding the
pieces of the play and

00:18:22.970 --> 00:18:24.340 align:middle line:90%
associative things.

00:18:24.340 --> 00:18:29.428 align:middle line:84%
So you find a hedgehog and you
find some text that makes a

00:18:29.428 --> 00:18:32.003 align:middle line:84%
reference, that somehow makes
you think of the hedgehog and

00:18:32.003 --> 00:18:32.635 align:middle line:84%
you put them together and you
actually start building a

00:18:32.635 --> 00:18:33.320 align:middle line:90%
visual puzzle.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:36.095 align:middle line:84%
So it's really a challenge
of making sense of the

00:18:36.095 --> 00:18:37.580 align:middle line:90%
significance of text.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:42.530 align:middle line:84%
It [UNINTELLIGIBLE] you and your
picture of Shakespeare's

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:45.030 align:middle line:84%
play, The Tempest, starts to
emerge through the gameplay.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:48.810 align:middle line:84%
So a story sort of appears that
way, the way a picture

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:50.630 align:middle line:90%
appears in a jigsaw puzzle.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:53.964 align:middle line:84%
So it's a game you play without
knowing, without ever

00:18:53.964 --> 00:18:55.530 align:middle line:90%
having seen it.

00:18:55.530 --> 00:18:59.320 align:middle line:84%
But part of the experience of
theater is the way stories

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:03.040 align:middle line:84%
emerge from different patterns
in the game.

00:19:03.040 --> 00:19:04.624 align:middle line:84%
We're still trying to make a
story [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:19:04.624 --> 00:19:05.874 align:middle line:90%
through exploration.

00:19:05.874 --> 00:19:10.050 align:middle line:90%


00:19:10.050 --> 00:19:13.100 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Maybe if I talk
about my career as coming--

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:14.960 align:middle line:84%
I think I come from
an area where we

00:19:14.960 --> 00:19:17.120 align:middle line:90%
make really bad games.

00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:21.266 align:middle line:84%
There's a saying that education
games are like

00:19:21.266 --> 00:19:22.210 align:middle line:90%
chocolate-covered broccoli.

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:23.630 align:middle line:84%
It's not healthy
and not sweet.

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:26.680 align:middle line:84%
And I actually come from a
background where teachers or

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:30.070 align:middle line:84%
educators are asked to build
games for somebody.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:31.430 align:middle line:90%
And what you have is contents.

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:35.490 align:middle line:84%
And I can say that, because I
think most of my colleagues

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:36.080 align:middle line:90%
seem to know better.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:37.770 align:middle line:84%
They thought, OK,
I have content.

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:40.052 align:middle line:84%
That content is good, and now I
just make do with a song and

00:19:40.052 --> 00:19:42.293 align:middle line:90%
the kids are going to love it.

00:19:42.293 --> 00:19:46.660 align:middle line:84%
And I think, as you all see,
Jeremy, we all know how quick

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:49.918 align:middle line:90%
kids realize it is not fun.

00:19:49.918 --> 00:19:55.468 align:middle line:84%
And I think there are tons of
bad games out there that ruin

00:19:55.468 --> 00:19:58.947 align:middle line:84%
the reputation of educational
games in a sense.

00:19:58.947 --> 00:20:02.080 align:middle line:84%
And coming here to MIT and
working with Scott and people

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:05.520 align:middle line:84%
like Dan, that I realized more,
the question is more,

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:09.498 align:middle line:84%
what makes us curious about
the problem, about the

00:20:09.498 --> 00:20:10.680 align:middle line:90%
learning content.

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:11.540 align:middle line:84%
Of course, we can have the
content of the game, something

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:12.850 align:middle line:90%
we don't even have to call it.

00:20:12.850 --> 00:20:14.090 align:middle line:90%
It's more abstract.

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:17.650 align:middle line:84%
We look for metaphors that are
kind of like, if you can

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:20.780 align:middle line:84%
understand how to be interested
in history without

00:20:20.780 --> 00:20:24.260 align:middle line:84%
really learning all the real
data maybe you learn in other

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.130 align:middle line:90%
history, but you get interested

00:20:26.130 --> 00:20:27.700 align:middle line:90%
in how history works.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:31.240 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's kind of like
what I learned here and I

00:20:31.240 --> 00:20:36.630 align:middle line:84%
realize now that it's really
useful, that we don't use the

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:38.860 align:middle line:84%
chocolate covered broccoli
any more.

00:20:38.860 --> 00:20:42.070 align:middle line:84%
We ask more about, what
do we really want?

00:20:42.070 --> 00:20:44.890 align:middle line:84%
Do we want to have something
that is healthy or sweet, or

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:47.340 align:middle line:84%
something different,
let's cook it.

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:49.500 align:middle line:84%
Let's not just combine
two things.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:53.930 align:middle line:84%
And I think there's something
that also-- the good

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:55.820 align:middle line:84%
educational games that
are out there are

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:59.180 align:middle line:90%
heading in that direction.

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:03.530 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Do you have
counter-examples from your

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:09.199 align:middle line:84%
work, of something that seemed
fruitful but then kind of

00:21:09.199 --> 00:21:13.095 align:middle line:84%
maybe you were trying to address
some subject area and

00:21:13.095 --> 00:21:16.922 align:middle line:84%
then were trying to marry some
specific game mechanics you

00:21:16.922 --> 00:21:19.180 align:middle line:84%
thought might work, but
were disappointed?

00:21:19.180 --> 00:21:21.710 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I could talk
about one that's not resolved

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:24.480 align:middle line:90%
yet, that I'm struggling with.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:27.350 align:middle line:90%


00:21:27.350 --> 00:21:31.081 align:middle line:84%
We're doing a game with a
professor here at Ocean, in

00:21:31.081 --> 00:21:34.810 align:middle line:84%
environmental engineering,
about microbes.

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:38.714 align:middle line:84%
He does all his work study
on microbes in the ocean.

00:21:38.714 --> 00:21:40.178 align:middle line:90%
Which I've learned about.

00:21:40.178 --> 00:21:42.630 align:middle line:84%
Half the biomass of the
ocean are microbes.

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:44.954 align:middle line:84%
And we know almost nothing
about them, because until

00:21:44.954 --> 00:21:48.745 align:middle line:84%
people like him, he's one of a
few, built apparatus to study

00:21:48.745 --> 00:21:51.520 align:middle line:84%
ocean-like conditions in the
laboratory, we had no way of

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:53.050 align:middle line:84%
knowing what was going
on with them.

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:56.140 align:middle line:84%
So that's interesting
if you get into it.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:58.703 align:middle line:84%
It's not something that
middle school kids are

00:21:58.703 --> 00:22:02.030 align:middle line:90%
dying to know about.

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:07.343 align:middle line:84%
So we're doing a simulation
where the premise is that

00:22:07.343 --> 00:22:11.584 align:middle line:84%
you've got a part of the ocean
that's dead and you're trying

00:22:11.584 --> 00:22:14.828 align:middle line:84%
to re-engineer it by introducing
nano-bots, which

00:22:14.828 --> 00:22:15.826 align:middle line:90%
you can build.

00:22:15.826 --> 00:22:19.818 align:middle line:84%
And these nano-bots would in
effect simulate the functions

00:22:19.818 --> 00:22:28.301 align:middle line:84%
of what looked like
processing oxygen.

00:22:28.301 --> 00:22:35.786 align:middle line:84%
Or rather, plants processing
either oxygen and producing

00:22:35.786 --> 00:22:38.090 align:middle line:84%
CO2 and energy, or
the reverse.

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:40.090 align:middle line:90%
Taking CO2 and sunlight and--

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:41.240 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:45.506 align:middle line:84%
So, the challenge, we made a
sort of interesting playable

00:22:45.506 --> 00:22:47.530 align:middle line:84%
simulation where you
build the stuff.

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:56.810 align:middle line:84%
But I haven't figured out yet
I'm struggling with, have we

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:00.369 align:middle line:84%
found a way to entice you to
do this, though, without

00:23:00.369 --> 00:23:01.770 align:middle line:84%
having to give you too
much information?

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:05.380 align:middle line:84%
And I'm a big believer that a
game-- this game does not

00:23:05.380 --> 00:23:09.420 align:middle line:84%
start with instructions, because
I don't believe games

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:10.875 align:middle line:84%
should ever start with
instructions.

00:23:10.875 --> 00:23:13.020 align:middle line:84%
But I'm still struggling with
the information we're

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:13.380 align:middle line:90%
providing along the way.

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:17.620 align:middle line:84%
There's too much read this now,
as opposed to-- and I

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:20.530 align:middle line:84%
think the problem is, because
simulations are very wide open

00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:23.390 align:middle line:84%
environments, and it's very hard
to constrain a simulation

00:23:23.390 --> 00:23:26.640 align:middle line:84%
enough that the player goes,
ah, now I understand what

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:28.764 align:middle line:90%
variable I just used.

00:23:28.764 --> 00:23:33.246 align:middle line:84%
And particularly because kids
have a tendency to see

00:23:33.246 --> 00:23:35.238 align:middle line:90%
simulations as black boxes.

00:23:35.238 --> 00:23:38.475 align:middle line:84%
So they'll change three
variables at once and not

00:23:38.475 --> 00:23:41.089 align:middle line:84%
notice they've changed three
variables, and not think about

00:23:41.089 --> 00:23:41.712 align:middle line:90%
what each variable was.

00:23:41.712 --> 00:23:45.480 align:middle line:84%
They just don't have the
practice of changing--

00:23:45.480 --> 00:23:47.105 align:middle line:84%
really, controlling
three variables.

00:23:47.105 --> 00:23:49.750 align:middle line:84%
And if you don't do that,
simulations do become black

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:52.530 align:middle line:84%
boxes, and I'm still struggling
with how to-- not

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:56.941 align:middle line:84%
to, say, mark the kid down the
road and say, OK, now make

00:23:56.941 --> 00:23:58.578 align:middle line:84%
this choice, now make
that choice.

00:23:58.578 --> 00:24:02.506 align:middle line:84%
Because that's too constrained
and not fun.

00:24:02.506 --> 00:24:06.311 align:middle line:84%
I want to have them have a lot
of choices but also have them

00:24:06.311 --> 00:24:08.398 align:middle line:84%
understand the effect of the
choices that they've made.

00:24:08.398 --> 00:24:10.362 align:middle line:84%
Maybe that's the best
way to sum it.

00:24:10.362 --> 00:24:12.817 align:middle line:84%
So just processing the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:24:12.817 --> 00:24:16.745 align:middle line:90%


00:24:16.745 --> 00:24:19.200 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I can give
a better example.

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:20.875 align:middle line:84%
So, I was working
on this game.

00:24:20.875 --> 00:24:23.480 align:middle line:84%
I wasn't even working on
that, to my excuse.

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:25.663 align:middle line:84%
I was beginning to work with
these people, then realized

00:24:25.663 --> 00:24:28.200 align:middle line:90%
it's not the way I want to go.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:30.600 align:middle line:90%
And then I stepped out of it.

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:31.930 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's a good
example of what

00:24:31.930 --> 00:24:32.770 align:middle line:90%
you shouldn't do.

00:24:32.770 --> 00:24:37.530 align:middle line:84%
So this small town in Austria
is using their waste to

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:40.620 align:middle line:90%
produce renewable energy.

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:41.435 align:middle line:90%
It's really interesting.

00:24:41.435 --> 00:24:45.030 align:middle line:84%
The town is somewhere in the
countryside and nobody was

00:24:45.030 --> 00:24:45.170 align:middle line:90%
interested.

00:24:45.170 --> 00:24:48.570 align:middle line:84%
It makes so much power now that
they can give power for

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:52.350 align:middle line:84%
free in their town to companies
and the neighboring

00:24:52.350 --> 00:24:54.690 align:middle line:84%
companies in neighboring
towns.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.370 align:middle line:84%
So a lot of people
are interested

00:24:56.370 --> 00:24:57.970 align:middle line:90%
in how they do that.

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:00.260 align:middle line:84%
And we thought, well, we want to
be a cool town, what if we

00:25:00.260 --> 00:25:03.870 align:middle line:84%
make a game instead of just
providing the content.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:07.630 align:middle line:84%
But what they did, they gave us
all the content and said,

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:08.830 align:middle line:84%
this is what you should
teach the people.

00:25:08.830 --> 00:25:11.950 align:middle line:90%
And make it fun.

00:25:11.950 --> 00:25:15.400 align:middle line:84%
And in the end, these people
were sitting together, OK,

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:16.670 align:middle line:90%
what do people like?

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:17.230 align:middle line:90%
Comics?

00:25:17.230 --> 00:25:20.110 align:middle line:84%
And you have to a character
that is moving around.

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:22.780 align:middle line:84%
And they would make small
multiple choice tests.

00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:27.860 align:middle line:84%
And afterwards they get their
rewards, and it says your name

00:25:27.860 --> 00:25:30.390 align:middle line:84%
and it says you're trained
in that discipline.

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:34.150 align:middle line:84%
And what was interesting for
me, they built the game.

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:35.600 align:middle line:90%
And the game's been bashed.

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:37.690 align:middle line:84%
And it's very sad and
it's horrible.

00:25:37.690 --> 00:25:41.030 align:middle line:84%
But the funniest thing for me
was, who was the target group?

00:25:41.030 --> 00:25:42.770 align:middle line:84%
And they never talked
about that.

00:25:42.770 --> 00:25:45.150 align:middle line:84%
They always thought who cares
about target groups, we make

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:47.545 align:middle line:84%
this game, everybody's going
to love it, everybody's so

00:25:47.545 --> 00:25:47.670 align:middle line:90%
interested.

00:25:47.670 --> 00:25:51.987 align:middle line:84%
The idea was who decides
if you can do that in

00:25:51.987 --> 00:25:53.420 align:middle line:90%
the majors of towns.

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:57.175 align:middle line:84%
They say, well, we want to
have a company like that.

00:25:57.175 --> 00:25:59.460 align:middle line:84%
And so now we're talking
about Austrian,

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:00.800 align:middle line:90%
European, majors of towns.

00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:03.640 align:middle line:84%
And they are normally fat
and like to drink wine.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:06.250 align:middle line:84%
Normally they sit with another
mayor and have lunch and then

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:07.620 align:middle line:90%
they say, wow, this works.

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:09.432 align:middle line:84%
And then they decide
to do it or not.

00:26:09.432 --> 00:26:12.190 align:middle line:84%
But now they were sending these
games to majors who did

00:26:12.190 --> 00:26:14.910 align:middle line:84%
not know at all what
to do with this.

00:26:14.910 --> 00:26:16.940 align:middle line:84%
They couldn't get
into the game.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.500 align:middle line:84%
And so, not that the
game was bad.

00:26:19.500 --> 00:26:21.670 align:middle line:84%
They didn't even think about
their target group.

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:27.260 align:middle line:84%
And I think that one aspect that
interested me the most

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:32.253 align:middle line:84%
is, you have to have the context
of the game as a

00:26:32.253 --> 00:26:32.505 align:middle line:90%
learning experience.

00:26:32.505 --> 00:26:33.830 align:middle line:90%
It has to be suitable.

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:35.270 align:middle line:84%
If this is not happening,
you can play the

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:37.030 align:middle line:90%
most interesting game.

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:40.870 align:middle line:84%
If someone doesn't get the
problem, doesn't get engaged

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:45.130 align:middle line:84%
to the problem, it's just
something happening.

00:26:45.130 --> 00:26:46.800 align:middle line:90%
It's not really interesting.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:49.790 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's a major
problem that this

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:55.200 align:middle line:90%
example was totally .

00:26:55.200 --> 00:26:59.610 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So, know who you're
designing your game for.

00:26:59.610 --> 00:27:02.870 align:middle line:84%
And how do you think they get
engaged in the games.

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:04.120 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:09.010 align:middle line:90%


00:27:09.010 --> 00:27:12.082 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Just to do a
simplistic example, but it's

00:27:12.082 --> 00:27:13.510 align:middle line:90%
one [UNINTELLIGIBLE] example.

00:27:13.510 --> 00:27:16.740 align:middle line:84%
If I wanted a game for my
mother, who occasionally would

00:27:16.740 --> 00:27:18.080 align:middle line:90%
play a casual game.

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:21.682 align:middle line:84%
I wouldn't do a massively
multiplayer game with lots of

00:27:21.682 --> 00:27:22.630 align:middle line:90%
stuff going around.

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:24.350 align:middle line:84%
All these senior citizens are
going to get into this

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:25.130 align:middle line:90%
massively multiplayer game.

00:27:25.130 --> 00:27:28.463 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it sounds like it would
be really great, to come

00:27:28.463 --> 00:27:29.005 align:middle line:84%
up with a massively multiplayer
game for senior

00:27:29.005 --> 00:27:29.890 align:middle line:84%
citizens, because of
their eyesight.

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:34.370 align:middle line:84%
But you also know that they
didn't grow up with computers,

00:27:34.370 --> 00:27:37.810 align:middle line:84%
and they're not going to be able
to navigate a 3D space.

00:27:37.810 --> 00:27:42.726 align:middle line:84%
So, that's a simplified example
of why it's important

00:27:42.726 --> 00:27:43.714 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:27:43.714 --> 00:27:46.925 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And what is really
good about it is that game

00:27:46.925 --> 00:27:49.148 align:middle line:84%
like [? Revish ?] the
cancer game, maybe

00:27:49.148 --> 00:27:51.124 align:middle line:90%
some of you know about.

00:27:51.124 --> 00:27:53.594 align:middle line:84%
It's a cancer game
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:27:53.594 --> 00:27:55.470 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: It was actually
mentioned in the reading

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:55.820 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:27:55.820 --> 00:27:58.164 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: But it's
hopefully one of the best

00:27:58.164 --> 00:27:59.130 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] learning
games out there.

00:27:59.130 --> 00:28:02.295 align:middle line:84%
But the good thing about them
is, people that play that game

00:28:02.295 --> 00:28:03.870 align:middle line:90%
have cancer.

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:07.970 align:middle line:84%
So you have the problem to the
game is so close that people

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:12.110 align:middle line:84%
who do not play the game say oh,
I don't care about cancer.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:13.645 align:middle line:84%
They care about cancer because
they have cancer.

00:28:13.645 --> 00:28:17.120 align:middle line:84%
The game is about cancer, and
the way chemotherapy works.

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:21.700 align:middle line:84%
So the context of the engagement
is automatically,

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:23.220 align:middle line:90%
the problem is solved.

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:25.912 align:middle line:90%
But what about this cancer.

00:28:25.912 --> 00:28:28.090 align:middle line:84%
What about a learning
game where it's

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:28.816 align:middle line:90%
about language learning?

00:28:28.816 --> 00:28:30.053 align:middle line:84%
Are these people already
interested

00:28:30.053 --> 00:28:31.000 align:middle line:90%
to learn the language?

00:28:31.000 --> 00:28:33.510 align:middle line:84%
Do they come there with their
language problem, or do we

00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:37.210 align:middle line:84%
want to introduce them to the
problem or show them maybe you

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:40.120 align:middle line:84%
don't have to be afraid to learn
a language or whatever.

00:28:40.120 --> 00:28:41.508 align:middle line:84%
This question has to
be addressed at

00:28:41.508 --> 00:28:42.670 align:middle line:90%
a very early stage.

00:28:42.670 --> 00:28:46.443 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, if you start
designing and then address the

00:28:46.443 --> 00:28:47.856 align:middle line:90%
question, it's late.

00:28:47.856 --> 00:28:50.860 align:middle line:90%


00:28:50.860 --> 00:28:55.036 align:middle line:84%
The question is sort of about
the explicit versus the

00:28:55.036 --> 00:28:55.964 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:28:55.964 --> 00:28:59.140 align:middle line:84%
So if you play [UNINTELLIGIBLE],
that was

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:04.556 align:middle line:84%
really designed to introduce,
basically, an idea that people

00:29:04.556 --> 00:29:07.780 align:middle line:84%
have struggled with, which are
what position and velocity

00:29:07.780 --> 00:29:09.380 align:middle line:90%
graphs actually mean.

00:29:09.380 --> 00:29:12.810 align:middle line:84%
But the game doesn't mention
velocity and it doesn't

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:13.550 align:middle line:90%
mention graphs.

00:29:13.550 --> 00:29:17.924 align:middle line:84%
And in fact in the same reviews
that it got lots of

00:29:17.924 --> 00:29:20.053 align:middle line:84%
good reviews when it came out,
a lot of them said, I had no

00:29:20.053 --> 00:29:21.303 align:middle line:90%
idea this was educational.

00:29:21.303 --> 00:29:24.000 align:middle line:90%


00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:27.200 align:middle line:84%
I wouldn't argue that that's
fine, that people don't

00:29:27.200 --> 00:29:30.728 align:middle line:84%
necessarily know that as they're
playing the game, that

00:29:30.728 --> 00:29:32.660 align:middle line:84%
they are still building up some
conceptual knowledge.

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:36.050 align:middle line:84%
I think that some large
percentage of the people who

00:29:36.050 --> 00:29:37.560 align:middle line:84%
play a game in that setting will
probably play the game

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:40.962 align:middle line:84%
and do nothing else but then
three months later

00:29:40.962 --> 00:29:41.001 align:middle line:90%
they'll have gone.

00:29:41.001 --> 00:29:42.251 align:middle line:84%
That experience for most people
will not be resilient.

00:29:42.251 --> 00:29:43.878 align:middle line:90%


00:29:43.878 --> 00:29:46.994 align:middle line:84%
Not because they didn't enjoy
it, but because they have lots

00:29:46.994 --> 00:29:49.610 align:middle line:84%
of things going on in life and
there's more information

00:29:49.610 --> 00:29:51.384 align:middle line:90%
coming out of [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:29:51.384 --> 00:29:53.986 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, the kid plays
that game and then you

00:29:53.986 --> 00:29:55.900 align:middle line:84%
sit down and have a conversation
about velocity

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:58.220 align:middle line:84%
and position graphs, there might
be some real meaningful

00:29:58.220 --> 00:29:59.192 align:middle line:90%
learning that occurs.

00:29:59.192 --> 00:30:01.740 align:middle line:84%
And so I think it's really
important to not overreach in

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:03.620 align:middle line:90%
that sense.

00:30:03.620 --> 00:30:06.508 align:middle line:84%
I try to avoid, although we
still do the saying that our

00:30:06.508 --> 00:30:07.496 align:middle line:90%
games teach.

00:30:07.496 --> 00:30:10.954 align:middle line:84%
Because I think teaching is a
much more active and explicit

00:30:10.954 --> 00:30:12.436 align:middle line:90%
activity to learn games too.

00:30:12.436 --> 00:30:16.060 align:middle line:84%
I think our games let people
play with them, let people

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:16.380 align:middle line:90%
learn from them.

00:30:16.380 --> 00:30:20.010 align:middle line:84%
And then just like if you
read a book, the book

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:20.632 align:middle line:90%
doesn't teach anything.

00:30:20.632 --> 00:30:22.998 align:middle line:84%
The book puts information
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:30:22.998 --> 00:30:27.853 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's really part
of, I think, we have to be

00:30:27.853 --> 00:30:31.464 align:middle line:84%
modest and humble about it
as we do this stuff.

00:30:31.464 --> 00:30:32.740 align:middle line:84%
But that was [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
as the challenge

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:33.340 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:36.500 align:middle line:84%
of thinking about, if you do
want some learning to occur,

00:30:36.500 --> 00:30:38.240 align:middle line:84%
where do you want the
learning to occur.

00:30:38.240 --> 00:30:41.560 align:middle line:84%
Is this for peoples' personal
lives, is it a mediated

00:30:41.560 --> 00:30:42.606 align:middle line:90%
setting like a hospital?

00:30:42.606 --> 00:30:46.890 align:middle line:84%
Or is it in a school--
those are really

00:30:46.890 --> 00:30:49.760 align:middle line:90%
critical questions .

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:52.876 align:middle line:84%
It's not just the learning
but the setting.

00:30:52.876 --> 00:30:55.834 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And who's introducing
a game to them,

00:30:55.834 --> 00:30:56.820 align:middle line:90%
for instance.

00:30:56.820 --> 00:30:59.090 align:middle line:90%
Is it a teacher, a doctor.

00:30:59.090 --> 00:30:59.840 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And were
they playing.

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:02.504 align:middle line:84%
Like with Labyrinth, which is
this middle school math game

00:31:02.504 --> 00:31:04.870 align:middle line:84%
that Dan and I were
working on.

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:09.790 align:middle line:84%
We knew that if we didn't push
in another direction, the

00:31:09.790 --> 00:31:12.440 align:middle line:84%
implementation was going to be,
well print this game and

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:14.074 align:middle line:90%
it gets played in classrooms.

00:31:14.074 --> 00:31:16.720 align:middle line:90%
If you've ever seen--

00:31:16.720 --> 00:31:20.342 align:middle line:84%
if you remember your own
computer labs in school, that

00:31:20.342 --> 00:31:23.057 align:middle line:84%
that was [UNINTELLIGIBLE], which
is true actually for a

00:31:23.057 --> 00:31:23.890 align:middle line:90%
lot of [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:31:23.890 --> 00:31:26.530 align:middle line:84%
It meant every now and then you
had to line up and walk

00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:29.165 align:middle line:84%
down the hall to this other
place, that you would only get

00:31:29.165 --> 00:31:30.150 align:middle line:90%
to occasionally.

00:31:30.150 --> 00:31:32.734 align:middle line:84%
And by the time you settled into
your chairs and booted it

00:31:32.734 --> 00:31:34.942 align:middle line:84%
up, you had maybe 20 minutes
on the computer and then it

00:31:34.942 --> 00:31:35.200 align:middle line:90%
was time to go.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:38.242 align:middle line:84%
So if you design something for
schools, you're talking about

00:31:38.242 --> 00:31:42.037 align:middle line:84%
a 20 minute experience once
every two or three weeks.

00:31:42.037 --> 00:31:44.370 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure that was
worth thinking about

00:31:44.370 --> 00:31:45.970 align:middle line:90%
anything for that.

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:49.555 align:middle line:84%
So we design the game, we
play it online at home.

00:31:49.555 --> 00:31:54.106 align:middle line:84%
And encourage teachers to let
the kids just play it.

00:31:54.106 --> 00:31:56.606 align:middle line:84%
And there was another advantage
of that, which was

00:31:56.606 --> 00:31:57.190 align:middle line:84%
to go back to who
introduces it.

00:31:57.190 --> 00:31:59.680 align:middle line:84%
If you play that-- even if we
have the perfect 20 minute

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:02.970 align:middle line:84%
game for the computer lab, if
the teacher's walking around--

00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:03.840 align:middle line:90%
I shouldn't say.

00:32:03.840 --> 00:32:05.560 align:middle line:84%
I've seen good uses
of that space.

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:07.610 align:middle line:84%
But there's some greater
probability you're going to

00:32:07.610 --> 00:32:09.290 align:middle line:84%
feel like this is
just a lesson.

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:11.430 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if the teacher says, go
play this at home, there's

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:13.876 align:middle line:84%
some greater probability that
you will sort of forget about

00:32:13.876 --> 00:32:15.656 align:middle line:84%
the school and get
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:32:15.656 --> 00:32:17.130 align:middle line:84%
And again, it's not that
we want to trick

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:18.225 align:middle line:90%
you into doing math.

00:32:18.225 --> 00:32:20.930 align:middle line:84%
It's that we want to engage
with the math in the most

00:32:20.930 --> 00:32:22.880 align:middle line:90%
pleasant spirit possible.

00:32:22.880 --> 00:32:27.836 align:middle line:84%
And the less we give the wrapper
of school to it, the

00:32:27.836 --> 00:32:29.808 align:middle line:84%
more playful you're
likely to be.

00:32:29.808 --> 00:32:32.766 align:middle line:84%
Even if you're not denying that
it's math or science or

00:32:32.766 --> 00:32:33.752 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:32:33.752 --> 00:32:37.696 align:middle line:84%
So that was
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].

00:32:37.696 --> 00:32:41.311 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So Constantine,
you had said the cancer game

00:32:41.311 --> 00:32:42.626 align:middle line:90%
is really well evaluated.

00:32:42.626 --> 00:32:45.816 align:middle line:84%
So did any of you have stories
or experiences from seeing

00:32:45.816 --> 00:32:48.714 align:middle line:84%
your games out in the
world that way.

00:32:48.714 --> 00:32:51.023 align:middle line:84%
You've gotten feedback from
teachers who have tried this

00:32:51.023 --> 00:32:52.273 align:middle line:90%
in the classrooms.

00:32:52.273 --> 00:32:57.910 align:middle line:90%


00:32:57.910 --> 00:32:59.890 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:32:59.890 --> 00:33:01.140 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:05.100 align:middle line:90%


00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:10.340 align:middle line:84%
so from the game I just talked
about, about renewable

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:12.720 align:middle line:90%
energies that totally failed.

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:16.780 align:middle line:84%
People played it but didn't
understand it, hated it, or

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:17.030 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:33:17.030 --> 00:33:20.290 align:middle line:90%
So I think, total failure.

00:33:20.290 --> 00:33:27.550 align:middle line:84%
And I think just to start with
this conversation is, this is

00:33:27.550 --> 00:33:30.190 align:middle line:90%
a problematic thing.

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:36.350 align:middle line:84%
Because there is very little
research going on on the

00:33:36.350 --> 00:33:39.380 align:middle line:84%
iteration of games, because
it's also very hard.

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:42.510 align:middle line:84%
It takes lot of effort and it
also could take a lot of money

00:33:42.510 --> 00:33:48.898 align:middle line:84%
to evaluate the research, that
the success of the game for

00:33:48.898 --> 00:33:51.343 align:middle line:90%
learning purposes.

00:33:51.343 --> 00:33:54.562 align:middle line:84%
And I just know very
few studies that do

00:33:54.562 --> 00:33:56.458 align:middle line:90%
that, in the end.

00:33:56.458 --> 00:34:00.490 align:middle line:84%
And I think we've just reached
that moment where people start

00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:02.210 align:middle line:84%
to think that this is an
interesting way to do it.

00:34:02.210 --> 00:34:07.020 align:middle line:84%
And Remission, as I said,
is probably--

00:34:07.020 --> 00:34:09.000 align:middle line:84%
maybe you'll correct me in a
second, but I think it's the

00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:12.300 align:middle line:84%
only game I would put my hand
into the fire for it.

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:16.397 align:middle line:84%
But the question is, what did
they learn in the game?

00:34:16.397 --> 00:34:17.891 align:middle line:84%
They learned about
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:34:17.891 --> 00:34:21.875 align:middle line:84%
They learned that there is
something they can do.

00:34:21.875 --> 00:34:25.361 align:middle line:84%
They learned a more positive
perspective, a more positive

00:34:25.361 --> 00:34:26.855 align:middle line:90%
relation to medicine.

00:34:26.855 --> 00:34:30.839 align:middle line:84%
They didn't learn
anything else.

00:34:30.839 --> 00:34:31.835 align:middle line:90%
In the research--

00:34:31.835 --> 00:34:33.827 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: It doesn't really
change their illness.

00:34:33.827 --> 00:34:35.422 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And they didn't
get happier.

00:34:35.422 --> 00:34:39.120 align:middle line:84%
It's not that they said, wow,
now, I couldn't cope better

00:34:39.120 --> 00:34:40.389 align:middle line:90%
with their illness.

00:34:40.389 --> 00:34:44.584 align:middle line:84%
They just took the medicine and
so the doctors were really

00:34:44.584 --> 00:34:44.960 align:middle line:90%
happy with that.

00:34:44.960 --> 00:34:46.961 align:middle line:90%
But--

00:34:46.961 --> 00:34:48.284 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: That's
the stated goal

00:34:48.284 --> 00:34:50.224 align:middle line:90%
of Remission, actually.

00:34:50.224 --> 00:34:55.151 align:middle line:84%
It's, this is a 3D game
where you play a

00:34:55.151 --> 00:34:57.541 align:middle line:90%
woman in space or something.

00:34:57.541 --> 00:35:01.050 align:middle line:90%
Shot down and [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:04.200 align:middle line:84%
but the stated goal of the game,
at least of the people

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:07.942 align:middle line:84%
who were making it, was to try
to encourage people who are

00:35:07.942 --> 00:35:12.566 align:middle line:84%
suffering from cancer to keep
up with their medication.

00:35:12.566 --> 00:35:18.960 align:middle line:84%
Don't fail keeping up the
schedule of taking your

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:22.320 align:middle line:90%
chemotherapy drugs, basically.

00:35:22.320 --> 00:35:25.617 align:middle line:84%
And the game was designed to
show what happens, what these

00:35:25.617 --> 00:35:30.487 align:middle line:84%
drugs actually try to do in some
sort of fictional-- or

00:35:30.487 --> 00:35:33.896 align:middle line:84%
fantastical, I'm not going
to say fictional --

00:35:33.896 --> 00:35:36.818 align:middle line:90%
so that's Remission.

00:35:36.818 --> 00:35:38.766 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Is this a free
play game as well?

00:35:38.766 --> 00:35:39.155 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:35:39.155 --> 00:35:39.253 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:35:39.253 --> 00:35:41.201 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:35:41.201 --> 00:35:43.675 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So, on Labyrinth
the original grant

00:35:43.675 --> 00:35:47.575 align:middle line:84%
include an excess of one year
evaluation on the game use.

00:35:47.575 --> 00:35:50.910 align:middle line:84%
And then when Congress cut back
the funding of the larger

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:55.362 align:middle line:84%
program, of which Labyrinth was
just one small piece, that

00:35:55.362 --> 00:35:55.605 align:middle line:90%
got cut off.

00:35:55.605 --> 00:35:57.306 align:middle line:84%
And so the research
didn't happen.

00:35:57.306 --> 00:36:00.222 align:middle line:84%
We're now looking for a grant,
because Labyrinth has been

00:36:00.222 --> 00:36:04.620 align:middle line:84%
used aggressively in Maryland
schools [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:05.560 align:middle line:84%
So we are going to
look for funding.

00:36:05.560 --> 00:36:07.690 align:middle line:84%
But generally, there's
less money.

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:09.280 align:middle line:84%
As little money as there is
you're developing this

00:36:09.280 --> 00:36:12.619 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] there's even
less for researching them.

00:36:12.619 --> 00:36:18.448 align:middle line:84%
And I think some of what I think
our work is designed

00:36:18.448 --> 00:36:20.547 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], we figure
out what we can learn by

00:36:20.547 --> 00:36:20.918 align:middle line:90%
making the game.

00:36:20.918 --> 00:36:22.760 align:middle line:84%
And we publish what we've
learned in the hope that other

00:36:22.760 --> 00:36:25.550 align:middle line:84%
people will pick up the ball,
people who have more of a

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:29.935 align:middle line:84%
research bent in the
traditional sense.

00:36:29.935 --> 00:36:32.266 align:middle line:84%
Will start to do the
research about it.

00:36:32.266 --> 00:36:32.960 align:middle line:84%
I think we're on the
brink of that.

00:36:32.960 --> 00:36:37.270 align:middle line:84%
I think with stuff like the
Quest to Learn School.

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:39.220 align:middle line:84%
Increasing numbers of
educators are using

00:36:39.220 --> 00:36:40.390 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:36:40.390 --> 00:36:44.568 align:middle line:84%
I do have only anecdotal
evidence, but the very first

00:36:44.568 --> 00:36:50.513 align:middle line:84%
game that I did, which was this
massive logic oriented

00:36:50.513 --> 00:36:54.490 align:middle line:84%
game, ended up being not
particularly well marketed.

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:57.870 align:middle line:84%
It nevertheless sold
a million copies.

00:36:57.870 --> 00:37:02.508 align:middle line:84%
And the anecdotal evidence I got
was that it was one of the

00:37:02.508 --> 00:37:04.240 align:middle line:84%
rare cases where parents would
bring it in and recommend it

00:37:04.240 --> 00:37:06.430 align:middle line:84%
to teachers, and teachers would
recommend it to parents.

00:37:06.430 --> 00:37:09.092 align:middle line:84%
Or kids would come home from
school recommending it.

00:37:09.092 --> 00:37:12.627 align:middle line:84%
And there seemed to be this
fairly broad acceptance by

00:37:12.627 --> 00:37:16.175 align:middle line:84%
both parents and teachers and
kids, without kids having the

00:37:16.175 --> 00:37:18.815 align:middle line:84%
sense that, oh, my teacher's
making me do this.

00:37:18.815 --> 00:37:20.265 align:middle line:84%
And I hear that now that
everybody's rich and famous in

00:37:20.265 --> 00:37:22.790 align:middle line:84%
their 20s, I occasionally
hear from them.

00:37:22.790 --> 00:37:25.774 align:middle line:84%
And some of them say it's
what interested

00:37:25.774 --> 00:37:26.590 align:middle line:90%
them in math and stuff.

00:37:26.590 --> 00:37:29.478 align:middle line:84%
So that's all anecdotal, and
maybe [UNINTELLIGIBLE] has

00:37:29.478 --> 00:37:30.728 align:middle line:90%
some [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:37:30.728 --> 00:37:33.220 align:middle line:90%


00:37:33.220 --> 00:37:36.131 align:middle line:84%
But I think the million copies
is beating towards the--

00:37:36.131 --> 00:37:37.381 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR:
[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:37:37.381 --> 00:37:46.280 align:middle line:90%


00:37:46.280 --> 00:37:46.600 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Sure.

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:48.405 align:middle line:84%
I'll just add a little
bit to that.

00:37:48.405 --> 00:37:51.830 align:middle line:84%
I also have some anecdotal
stuff I want to bring in.

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:54.481 align:middle line:90%


00:37:54.481 --> 00:37:57.420 align:middle line:84%
But the most interesting thing
that I've discovered was with

00:37:57.420 --> 00:38:01.660 align:middle line:84%
Labyrinth and a target audience
that I've seen play

00:38:01.660 --> 00:38:05.053 align:middle line:84%
it, that it was not specifically
designed for.

00:38:05.053 --> 00:38:11.040 align:middle line:84%
Which is kids with some sort
of attention disorder.

00:38:11.040 --> 00:38:16.284 align:middle line:84%
Whether it's ADD, or autism
or something.

00:38:16.284 --> 00:38:19.845 align:middle line:84%
And seeing these kids engage
with the game, even engage

00:38:19.845 --> 00:38:25.148 align:middle line:84%
with the game in a social
way, and be

00:38:25.148 --> 00:38:27.608 align:middle line:90%
hyperfocused on the game.

00:38:27.608 --> 00:38:34.870 align:middle line:84%
And be highly competent with the
game, and to really calm

00:38:34.870 --> 00:38:40.978 align:middle line:84%
down and adopt pro-social
behaviors that were not

00:38:40.978 --> 00:38:43.816 align:middle line:90%
characteristic of them.

00:38:43.816 --> 00:38:46.912 align:middle line:84%
Or that their peers
or families hadn't

00:38:46.912 --> 00:38:48.265 align:middle line:90%
seen with them before.

00:38:48.265 --> 00:38:51.211 align:middle line:84%
I think there's a lot
of power there.

00:38:51.211 --> 00:38:54.036 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: The first two
teachers I've met who have

00:38:54.036 --> 00:38:57.308 align:middle line:84%
used Labyrinth said they were
seeing performance with kids

00:38:57.308 --> 00:39:01.204 align:middle line:84%
they didn't expect
to see before.

00:39:01.204 --> 00:39:04.249 align:middle line:84%
Because our sense was that lots
of kids have competencies

00:39:04.249 --> 00:39:05.398 align:middle line:84%
and skills that they
don't show off in

00:39:05.398 --> 00:39:07.020 align:middle line:90%
the format of school.

00:39:07.020 --> 00:39:10.780 align:middle line:84%
School being its own kind of
game which only certain people

00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:11.680 align:middle line:90%
are good at.

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:15.713 align:middle line:84%
And so having a kid be able to
demonstrate a competency at a

00:39:15.713 --> 00:39:18.096 align:middle line:84%
different kind of game, and
let the teacher see it.

00:39:18.096 --> 00:39:21.634 align:middle line:84%
Because part of the design of
Labyrinth is that teachers can

00:39:21.634 --> 00:39:22.980 align:middle line:90%
track progress online.

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:24.483 align:middle line:90%
That was one of our goals.

00:39:24.483 --> 00:39:26.948 align:middle line:90%
And that was [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:39:26.948 --> 00:39:28.427 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: This is a very
good laptop actually.

00:39:28.427 --> 00:39:30.892 align:middle line:84%
So is it possible to look it up
and show some of Labyrinth?

00:39:30.892 --> 00:39:33.850 align:middle line:84%
Because we've been talking
about it in this aspect.

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:34.343 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, sure.

00:39:34.343 --> 00:39:34.836 align:middle line:90%
Absolutely.

00:39:34.836 --> 00:39:36.808 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:39:36.808 --> 00:39:40.259 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: It's just
a notion of a game.

00:39:40.259 --> 00:39:42.231 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: But other people
can [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:39:42.231 --> 00:39:43.481 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:39:43.481 --> 00:39:48.500 align:middle line:90%


00:39:48.500 --> 00:39:51.780 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: While we're setting
that up, I'd like to ask a bit

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:53.218 align:middle line:90%
about prototyping.

00:39:53.218 --> 00:39:54.468 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:39:54.468 --> 00:40:11.182 align:middle line:90%


00:40:11.182 --> 00:40:15.500 align:middle line:84%
So, in the process of
designing-- these are mostly

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:17.875 align:middle line:84%
digital games that we've been
talking about all the time.

00:40:17.875 --> 00:40:21.130 align:middle line:84%
But I'd like to talk about the
early stage, when you're

00:40:21.130 --> 00:40:24.686 align:middle line:84%
working with paper or simple
digital prototypes.

00:40:24.686 --> 00:40:28.910 align:middle line:90%


00:40:28.910 --> 00:40:32.600 align:middle line:84%
Maybe you can talk a little bit
about how that might be

00:40:32.600 --> 00:40:37.375 align:middle line:84%
similar or different from
designing other kinds of

00:40:37.375 --> 00:40:40.504 align:middle line:84%
computer games, or
even board games?

00:40:40.504 --> 00:40:43.303 align:middle line:84%
Do you test it differently
because you're designing it

00:40:43.303 --> 00:40:45.938 align:middle line:84%
explicitly for a specific
audience?

00:40:45.938 --> 00:40:49.643 align:middle line:84%
Do you test it with instructors
as well as

00:40:49.643 --> 00:40:50.890 align:middle line:90%
players, or [UNINTELLIGIBLE]?

00:40:50.890 --> 00:40:52.960 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I think it's
always best to try to test

00:40:52.960 --> 00:40:54.660 align:middle line:90%
with your target audience.

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:57.230 align:middle line:84%
The things you'd learn
from [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:40:57.230 --> 00:41:00.810 align:middle line:90%


00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:03.230 align:middle line:84%
there's thing you learn, you can
learn from any audience.

00:41:03.230 --> 00:41:06.610 align:middle line:84%
Particularly usability,
which is important.

00:41:06.610 --> 00:41:11.004 align:middle line:84%
Frequently the flaw in a game is
not the core mechanic, but

00:41:11.004 --> 00:41:14.665 align:middle line:84%
the ease with which the player
can understand what they're

00:41:14.665 --> 00:41:14.950 align:middle line:90%
supposed to do.

00:41:14.950 --> 00:41:18.070 align:middle line:84%
But you can test that with
any wide range of people.

00:41:18.070 --> 00:41:20.660 align:middle line:84%
Anyone who you think is roughly
as computer game

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:23.090 align:middle line:84%
literate as your target
audience.

00:41:23.090 --> 00:41:27.500 align:middle line:84%
But I think if your goal is to
interest middle-school kids

00:41:27.500 --> 00:41:31.324 align:middle line:84%
like we do with this game--
let me just turn this on--

00:41:31.324 --> 00:41:35.640 align:middle line:90%


00:41:35.640 --> 00:41:37.640 align:middle line:84%
then you do want to, because
there are questions about age

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:40.230 align:middle line:90%
appropriateness.

00:41:40.230 --> 00:41:42.598 align:middle line:90%
Or [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE]

00:41:42.598 --> 00:41:51.030 align:middle line:90%


00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:51.526 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:41:51.526 --> 00:41:53.900 align:middle line:84%
So we're about to get
this screen here.

00:41:53.900 --> 00:41:55.640 align:middle line:90%
So this is a login screen.

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:59.610 align:middle line:84%
This is not the first screen
you would come to if you'd

00:41:59.610 --> 00:42:03.530 align:middle line:84%
started the game from scratch,
you get some more information.

00:42:03.530 --> 00:42:04.090 align:middle line:90%
But I've already logged in.

00:42:04.090 --> 00:42:08.010 align:middle line:90%
So what you'll notice--

00:42:08.010 --> 00:42:11.810 align:middle line:90%
let me just log in.

00:42:11.810 --> 00:42:12.780 align:middle line:90%
So it's online.

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:14.080 align:middle line:84%
You can play from
any computer.

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:15.472 align:middle line:84%
That was important because
we were concerned about

00:42:15.472 --> 00:42:18.820 align:middle line:84%
underserved populations who
either don't have computers at

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:24.120 align:middle line:84%
home or who have less control
over their time, don't have a

00:42:24.120 --> 00:42:25.510 align:middle line:84%
computer in their
room, certainly.

00:42:25.510 --> 00:42:27.590 align:middle line:84%
Or might do all their work
in an after-school

00:42:27.590 --> 00:42:29.310 align:middle line:90%
setting or a library.

00:42:29.310 --> 00:42:33.565 align:middle line:84%
So basically, once you
have an identity you

00:42:33.565 --> 00:42:35.002 align:middle line:90%
can log in from anywhere.

00:42:35.002 --> 00:42:37.397 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to briefly enter
the game space but

00:42:37.397 --> 00:42:38.647 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:42:38.647 --> 00:42:41.720 align:middle line:90%


00:42:41.720 --> 00:42:43.666 align:middle line:84%
So here we're already in
the middle of the game.

00:42:43.666 --> 00:42:45.840 align:middle line:84%
This is a puzzle
adventure game.

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:48.660 align:middle line:84%
And the plot is, someone's
stolen my pet.

00:42:48.660 --> 00:42:51.110 align:middle line:84%
I've tracked it down to this
underground factory that's

00:42:51.110 --> 00:42:52.790 align:middle line:90%
being run by monsters--

00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:55.170 align:middle line:90%
and there's middle schoolers--

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:58.912 align:middle line:84%
with the help of a mysterious
woman with

00:42:58.912 --> 00:43:01.620 align:middle line:90%
bluish skin and wings.

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:04.275 align:middle line:84%
I've been given a monster
costume, so in the guise of a

00:43:04.275 --> 00:43:10.070 align:middle line:84%
monster myself I can go around
and do jobs in the factory.

00:43:10.070 --> 00:43:12.838 align:middle line:84%
Find my pet, figure out what the
monsters are up to, which

00:43:12.838 --> 00:43:15.720 align:middle line:90%
is indeed no good.

00:43:15.720 --> 00:43:18.800 align:middle line:84%
And I'm in the middle of
this game, so I've

00:43:18.800 --> 00:43:19.090 align:middle line:90%
already advanced some.

00:43:19.090 --> 00:43:22.275 align:middle line:84%
There is a sequence of rooms,
which, part of the game is

00:43:22.275 --> 00:43:24.750 align:middle line:84%
just finding the rooms where
the puzzles are.

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:29.700 align:middle line:84%
For example, we've
got it here.

00:43:29.700 --> 00:43:33.165 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the wings
of the factory.

00:43:33.165 --> 00:43:35.640 align:middle line:90%
There's a hundred doors here.

00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:38.115 align:middle line:84%
And I can click on every
door if I have to.

00:43:38.115 --> 00:43:40.598 align:middle line:84%
But I have a map, which is
itself a mathematical tool, to

00:43:40.598 --> 00:43:44.930 align:middle line:90%
find where I want to go.

00:43:44.930 --> 00:43:48.750 align:middle line:84%
And as I go through, each room
represents a different puzzle.

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:50.480 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, having said
that, we're going

00:43:50.480 --> 00:43:51.260 align:middle line:90%
to actually go back--

00:43:51.260 --> 00:43:52.610 align:middle line:90%
And oh.

00:43:52.610 --> 00:44:00.135 align:middle line:84%
The other big feature in the
game is that if you play on

00:44:00.135 --> 00:44:02.350 align:middle line:84%
teams, then you can communicate
with your

00:44:02.350 --> 00:44:04.590 align:middle line:84%
teammates through this
communicator.

00:44:04.590 --> 00:44:07.730 align:middle line:90%


00:44:07.730 --> 00:44:09.933 align:middle line:84%
And the reason there is to
actually get kids reading and

00:44:09.933 --> 00:44:12.380 align:middle line:84%
writing about what they're
doing with math.

00:44:12.380 --> 00:44:14.300 align:middle line:84%
Because if you want to help
your teammmates, you can't

00:44:14.300 --> 00:44:16.690 align:middle line:84%
feed each other the answers
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:44:16.690 --> 00:44:17.940 align:middle line:90%
puzzle.

00:44:17.940 --> 00:44:20.310 align:middle line:90%


00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:21.596 align:middle line:84%
Every puzzle, every
time you play,

00:44:21.596 --> 00:44:22.730 align:middle line:90%
it's a different solution.

00:44:22.730 --> 00:44:25.474 align:middle line:84%
So you can't tell each other the
answer, all you can do is

00:44:25.474 --> 00:44:28.180 align:middle line:90%
talk about your strategies.

00:44:28.180 --> 00:44:30.116 align:middle line:84%
We're going to leave the game
and go to puzzle mode

00:44:30.116 --> 00:44:31.366 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:44:31.366 --> 00:44:40.540 align:middle line:90%


00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:42.140 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: This is a shortcut?

00:44:42.140 --> 00:44:44.068 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, shortcut
to puzzles, right.

00:44:44.068 --> 00:44:45.556 align:middle line:84%
You don't have to play it in
the context of the game.

00:44:45.556 --> 00:44:46.806 align:middle line:84%
You can play any puzzle
that you want.

00:44:46.806 --> 00:44:50.870 align:middle line:90%


00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:53.248 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to try one that
I've never played

00:44:53.248 --> 00:44:54.220 align:middle line:90%
with a group before.

00:44:54.220 --> 00:44:57.862 align:middle line:84%
So usually I pick the same game,
play it, and get used to

00:44:57.862 --> 00:44:58.594 align:middle line:90%
playing it with people.

00:44:58.594 --> 00:45:01.024 align:middle line:90%
But let's just try this one.

00:45:01.024 --> 00:45:02.968 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:45:02.968 --> 00:45:07.360 align:middle line:90%
I think I've tried level one.

00:45:07.360 --> 00:45:09.858 align:middle line:84%
So I'm trying to remember
what you know here.

00:45:09.858 --> 00:45:13.826 align:middle line:84%
You're in the back in
the shipping area.

00:45:13.826 --> 00:45:17.720 align:middle line:84%
And you've got to come up with
a manifest to ship the number

00:45:17.720 --> 00:45:19.234 align:middle line:90%
of objects that you've got.

00:45:19.234 --> 00:45:21.720 align:middle line:90%
That's all you know.

00:45:21.720 --> 00:45:22.980 align:middle line:84%
You've basically got to
give them the right

00:45:22.980 --> 00:45:25.325 align:middle line:90%
instructions to ship it.

00:45:25.325 --> 00:45:31.100 align:middle line:84%
And so now, just tell
me what to do here.

00:45:31.100 --> 00:45:33.950 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Click something.

00:45:33.950 --> 00:45:34.750 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Of course.

00:45:34.750 --> 00:45:37.620 align:middle line:90%
And that's [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:37.620 --> 00:45:38.300 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:38.300 --> 00:45:39.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:45:39.550 --> 00:45:42.020 align:middle line:90%


00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:43.890 align:middle line:90%
Up into the slots up there.

00:45:43.890 --> 00:45:46.720 align:middle line:90%
There's ones and zeroes.

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:49.578 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Can we put that
crazy fish head second to the

00:45:49.578 --> 00:45:52.566 align:middle line:84%
right on every one in
the equals spot?

00:45:52.566 --> 00:45:53.064 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

00:45:53.064 --> 00:45:54.558 align:middle line:90%
You can.

00:45:54.558 --> 00:45:56.550 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:45:56.550 --> 00:45:59.040 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Want to try
it in the equals spot.

00:45:59.040 --> 00:46:03.540 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And go to
the equals on this one.

00:46:03.540 --> 00:46:04.110 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:05.735 align:middle line:84%
Just tell us what your
thinking was.

00:46:05.735 --> 00:46:07.116 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, it looks
like we're trying

00:46:07.116 --> 00:46:08.970 align:middle line:90%
to build math equations.

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:13.896 align:middle line:84%
And usually they end with
equals something.

00:46:13.896 --> 00:46:14.380 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Right.

00:46:14.380 --> 00:46:15.230 align:middle line:90%
And you saw that.

00:46:15.230 --> 00:46:17.100 align:middle line:84%
Now, just so you know, the
equation we're going to

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:21.190 align:middle line:84%
build-- you're right, and just
as a shortcut-- is not about

00:46:21.190 --> 00:46:21.348 align:middle line:90%
arithmetic.

00:46:21.348 --> 00:46:22.056 align:middle line:90%
The equations are simple.

00:46:22.056 --> 00:46:23.708 align:middle line:84%
I'm not interested in drilling
kids on arithmetic.

00:46:23.708 --> 00:46:28.730 align:middle line:84%
This is really about number
systems, and number systems

00:46:28.730 --> 00:46:29.910 align:middle line:90%
are just simple systems.

00:46:29.910 --> 00:46:32.285 align:middle line:84%
But there's also some algebra
that's going to go on this, we

00:46:32.285 --> 00:46:34.030 align:middle line:90%
start to do this too.

00:46:34.030 --> 00:46:35.968 align:middle line:84%
And then in higher levels of the
puzzle, [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:46:35.968 --> 00:46:39.160 align:middle line:84%
you start doing stuff in
base two and base six.

00:46:39.160 --> 00:46:43.100 align:middle line:84%
We sort of dovetailed with
the notion that our

00:46:43.100 --> 00:46:45.110 align:middle line:84%
representations of numbers
are just symbolic.

00:46:45.110 --> 00:46:49.595 align:middle line:84%
So zero, or one, or one
zero, doesn't have

00:46:49.595 --> 00:46:51.040 align:middle line:90%
to always mean 10.

00:46:51.040 --> 00:46:52.810 align:middle line:90%
It's just a symbol.

00:46:52.810 --> 00:46:53.303 align:middle line:90%
Anyway.

00:46:53.303 --> 00:46:53.796 align:middle line:90%
So now what should we do?

00:46:53.796 --> 00:46:56.261 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Pac-Man
should be minus.

00:46:56.261 --> 00:47:01.191 align:middle line:84%
Because the only ones with six
digits have Pac-Man as the

00:47:01.191 --> 00:47:01.684 align:middle line:90%
third digit.

00:47:01.684 --> 00:47:03.163 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Sounds good.

00:47:03.163 --> 00:47:04.220 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm not sure
which one is Pac-Man.

00:47:04.220 --> 00:47:05.670 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Bottom right.

00:47:05.670 --> 00:47:06.150 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: This guy?

00:47:06.150 --> 00:47:07.590 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That guy's Pac-Man.

00:47:07.590 --> 00:47:10.950 align:middle line:90%
Pac-Man with an underline.

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:13.350 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I'm not sure
if we can actually

00:47:13.350 --> 00:47:16.230 align:middle line:90%
put that one there.

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:17.480 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:47:17.480 --> 00:47:19.310 align:middle line:90%


00:47:19.310 --> 00:47:22.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you look at the
top left, then what is the

00:47:22.730 --> 00:47:24.650 align:middle line:84%
number two digit number that,
minus the last digit, gives a

00:47:24.650 --> 00:47:26.119 align:middle line:90%
single-digit number.

00:47:26.119 --> 00:47:27.995 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:47:27.995 --> 00:47:30.340 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:47:30.340 --> 00:47:32.340 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, that's
a good point, yeah.

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:34.220 align:middle line:84%
It has to be divided by
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:47:34.220 --> 00:47:35.420 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: But now what
you're really doing is

00:47:35.420 --> 00:47:38.480 align:middle line:84%
thinking about the structure
of math.

00:47:38.480 --> 00:47:40.160 align:middle line:90%
Which is exactly what--

00:47:40.160 --> 00:47:44.710 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So when you
say that's divided--

00:47:44.710 --> 00:47:48.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So, I'm going to
guess the banana is four?

00:47:48.610 --> 00:47:50.530 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:47:50.530 --> 00:47:52.420 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: You
want to say why?

00:47:52.420 --> 00:47:55.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because using that
same logic, that first

00:47:55.940 --> 00:48:01.152 align:middle line:84%
version, that something, n x
divided by x equals some

00:48:01.152 --> 00:48:02.598 align:middle line:90%
single digit number.

00:48:02.598 --> 00:48:05.490 align:middle line:90%
And I'm guessing x is 4.

00:48:05.490 --> 00:48:05.980 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:48:05.980 --> 00:48:10.830 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: By the way, you
guys have avoided, and not

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:13.213 align:middle line:84%
noticed a piece of information
which would make your work

00:48:13.213 --> 00:48:16.010 align:middle line:90%
even more--

00:48:16.010 --> 00:48:18.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, the thing
at the top.

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:20.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's the
thing at the top?

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:23.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The thing
at the bottom.

00:48:23.050 --> 00:48:24.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It could be two,
actually, as well.

00:48:24.640 --> 00:48:25.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What are all
the green things?

00:48:25.830 --> 00:48:28.506 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Those are the
things you're shipping.

00:48:28.506 --> 00:48:29.850 align:middle line:84%
Those are jars of stuff,
of pepper.

00:48:29.850 --> 00:48:30.860 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, those
make sense.

00:48:30.860 --> 00:48:33.570 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Banana something
banana.

00:48:33.570 --> 00:48:34.892 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Banana
something--

00:48:34.892 --> 00:48:38.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Equals something
banana.

00:48:38.510 --> 00:48:38.870 align:middle line:90%
So--

00:48:38.870 --> 00:48:40.120 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:48:40.120 --> 00:48:44.300 align:middle line:90%


00:48:44.300 --> 00:48:45.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Banana could
actually be two.

00:48:45.670 --> 00:48:47.500 align:middle line:84%
Because 12 divided by two
is equal to six. six.

00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:48.750 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:48:48.750 --> 00:48:57.330 align:middle line:90%


00:48:57.330 --> 00:49:00.580 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, and in that
respect, that means a little--

00:49:00.580 --> 00:49:02.610 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, the four
squares are signs.

00:49:02.610 --> 00:49:03.580 align:middle line:90%
You try that?

00:49:03.580 --> 00:49:04.420 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:49:04.420 --> 00:49:06.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It has
to be zero on--

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:08.070 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Every time
you play the game

00:49:08.070 --> 00:49:08.530 align:middle line:90%
the symbols are different.

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:10.270 align:middle line:84%
They're the same symbols but
they're a different--

00:49:10.270 --> 00:49:11.640 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Triple backlash
should be-- oh.

00:49:11.640 --> 00:49:12.890 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:49:12.890 --> 00:49:15.200 align:middle line:90%


00:49:15.200 --> 00:49:16.180 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: By the way,
you're still missing a piece

00:49:16.180 --> 00:49:16.545 align:middle line:90%
of information.

00:49:16.545 --> 00:49:17.295 align:middle line:90%
But that's OK.

00:49:17.295 --> 00:49:19.050 align:middle line:84%
That's what games
are all about.

00:49:19.050 --> 00:49:19.440 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:20.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So what's this
thing in the top left?

00:49:20.850 --> 00:49:24.902 align:middle line:84%
Can we put things in there and
press enter, and will it

00:49:24.902 --> 00:49:26.878 align:middle line:90%
evaluate them for us?

00:49:26.878 --> 00:49:30.555 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, but right
now-- you're not done yet .

00:49:30.555 --> 00:49:31.805 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:49:31.805 --> 00:49:33.670 align:middle line:90%


00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:36.730 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, triple dash
is definitely one.

00:49:36.730 --> 00:49:39.530 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Triple
dash is--

00:49:39.530 --> 00:49:41.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, triple dash
is one, because one

00:49:41.510 --> 00:49:44.660 align:middle line:90%
times banana is banana.

00:49:44.660 --> 00:49:46.490 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Unless
banana's zero.

00:49:46.490 --> 00:49:47.740 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:54.210 align:middle line:90%


00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:56.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Banana's
six, I think.

00:49:56.050 --> 00:49:56.300 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It could be five.

00:49:56.300 --> 00:49:57.400 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I've never
done this game before--

00:49:57.400 --> 00:49:58.240 align:middle line:90%
no.

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:00.315 align:middle line:84%
The fact that you're naming
all these symbols, because

00:50:00.315 --> 00:50:02.080 align:middle line:90%
they were just symbols.

00:50:02.080 --> 00:50:03.330 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:50:03.330 --> 00:50:09.900 align:middle line:90%


00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:10.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I think banana's

00:50:10.430 --> 00:50:11.670 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE], because banana--

00:50:11.670 --> 00:50:13.090 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:50:13.090 --> 00:50:15.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
banana squared is equal to

00:50:15.260 --> 00:50:16.735 align:middle line:90%
something times banana.

00:50:16.735 --> 00:50:18.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Not something
times--

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:19.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So, yeah, it
could be 25 actually.

00:50:19.750 --> 00:50:21.440 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:50:21.440 --> 00:50:23.260 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Remember, I
didn't know the solution to

00:50:23.260 --> 00:50:25.951 align:middle line:84%
