00:50:25.951 --> 00:50:27.600 align:middle line:90%
know that you're wrong.

00:50:27.600 --> 00:50:28.810 align:middle line:90%
It's on the screen.

00:50:28.810 --> 00:50:30.030 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Banana
definitely is--

00:50:30.030 --> 00:50:31.280 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:50:31.280 --> 00:50:33.490 align:middle line:90%


00:50:33.490 --> 00:50:38.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Five because banana
squared is anchor banana.

00:50:38.980 --> 00:50:45.450 align:middle line:84%
But flower banana divided by
banana is also an integer.

00:50:45.450 --> 00:50:49.930 align:middle line:84%
So if flower banana and anchor
banana are local banana, then

00:50:49.930 --> 00:50:52.540 align:middle line:84%
banana should be five
and not six.

00:50:52.540 --> 00:50:56.530 align:middle line:84%
because flower minus anchor
times banana.

00:50:56.530 --> 00:50:58.910 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I love your
mental calculation.

00:50:58.910 --> 00:51:01.110 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:51:01.110 --> 00:51:02.805 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So you're
saying it's what?

00:51:02.805 --> 00:51:04.460 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Five.

00:51:04.460 --> 00:51:05.930 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Well, that could
work, but you could have

00:51:05.930 --> 00:51:07.550 align:middle line:90%
done it in a simpler way.

00:51:07.550 --> 00:51:10.270 align:middle line:84%
Or you could have surmised
it, not known it, but--

00:51:10.270 --> 00:51:12.230 align:middle line:84%
remember, this is only the
first time you played,

00:51:12.230 --> 00:51:14.140 align:middle line:84%
subsequent times you'd have
other information.

00:51:14.140 --> 00:51:15.390 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:51:15.390 --> 00:51:25.320 align:middle line:90%


00:51:25.320 --> 00:51:27.379 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: You're a member
of the group, so don't give

00:51:27.379 --> 00:51:28.730 align:middle line:90%
away [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:51:28.730 --> 00:51:31.100 align:middle line:90%
but in fact you're making--

00:51:31.100 --> 00:51:32.350 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:51:32.350 --> 00:51:35.370 align:middle line:90%


00:51:35.370 --> 00:51:36.678 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Anchor is 20.

00:51:36.678 --> 00:51:37.902 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Anchor is two.

00:51:37.902 --> 00:51:38.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sorry, two.

00:51:38.680 --> 00:51:41.100 align:middle line:90%
Not 20.

00:51:41.100 --> 00:51:42.970 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Anchor's two.

00:51:42.970 --> 00:51:46.920 align:middle line:90%
Claw is zero.

00:51:46.920 --> 00:51:47.660 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Where's claw?

00:51:47.660 --> 00:51:50.940 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Claw's the one
on the top left.

00:51:50.940 --> 00:51:52.140 align:middle line:90%
Of the squares that are left.

00:51:52.140 --> 00:51:52.840 align:middle line:90%
Left.

00:51:52.840 --> 00:51:53.700 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, you
mean brass circles.

00:51:53.700 --> 00:51:54.950 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:51:54.950 --> 00:51:58.150 align:middle line:90%


00:51:58.150 --> 00:51:58.995 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, that one.

00:51:58.995 --> 00:51:59.670 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's zero.

00:51:59.670 --> 00:52:01.870 align:middle line:90%
Has to be zero.

00:52:01.870 --> 00:52:02.990 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Wait,
why is it zero?

00:52:02.990 --> 00:52:04.480 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because if
you look at the

00:52:04.480 --> 00:52:05.320 align:middle line:90%
equation at the bottom--

00:52:05.320 --> 00:52:06.630 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, I'm looking
at the wrong spot.

00:52:06.630 --> 00:52:07.880 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:07.880 --> 00:52:24.710 align:middle line:90%


00:52:24.710 --> 00:52:26.250 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, sigma's nine.

00:52:26.250 --> 00:52:27.500 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:52:27.500 --> 00:52:33.610 align:middle line:90%


00:52:33.610 --> 00:52:36.050 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: OK, so
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:52:36.050 --> 00:52:40.070 align:middle line:90%
scythe is plus.

00:52:40.070 --> 00:52:42.340 align:middle line:90%
Scythe is plus.

00:52:42.340 --> 00:52:44.550 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: No, it has
to be minus.

00:52:44.550 --> 00:52:45.800 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:52:45.800 --> 00:53:27.069 align:middle line:90%


00:53:27.069 --> 00:53:29.110 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Anyone who is
having trouble, and this

00:53:29.110 --> 00:53:32.310 align:middle line:90%
happens in a group.

00:53:32.310 --> 00:53:33.210 align:middle line:84%
So, anyone having trouble
following it as people were

00:53:33.210 --> 00:53:35.270 align:middle line:84%
making stuff up or
finding stuff.

00:53:35.270 --> 00:53:36.205 align:middle line:90%
Because if you did it's OK.

00:53:36.205 --> 00:53:40.070 align:middle line:84%
In fact, sometimes you've got to
play the game yourself for

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:40.970 align:middle line:90%
it to make sense.

00:53:40.970 --> 00:53:41.970 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:53:41.970 --> 00:53:43.210 align:middle line:90%
I could have told you--

00:53:43.210 --> 00:53:46.937 align:middle line:84%
I actually think I could have
told you a lot about the game

00:53:46.937 --> 00:53:47.080 align:middle line:90%
going into it.

00:53:47.080 --> 00:53:48.590 align:middle line:84%
And there would still be a high
probability that when you

00:53:48.590 --> 00:53:51.030 align:middle line:84%
started playing it you would
have not absorbed

00:53:51.030 --> 00:53:52.530 align:middle line:90%
anything I'd said.

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:53.440 align:middle line:90%
That's my experience.

00:53:53.440 --> 00:53:55.893 align:middle line:84%
I could almost give it all away
but you still don't get

00:53:55.893 --> 00:53:56.310 align:middle line:90%
it until they play it.

00:53:56.310 --> 00:53:58.260 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So they
didn't know--

00:53:58.260 --> 00:53:59.410 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:53:59.410 --> 00:54:01.194 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Beta delta upside
down is eight.

00:54:01.194 --> 00:54:02.444 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:54:02.444 --> 00:54:08.906 align:middle line:90%


00:54:08.906 --> 00:54:12.690 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can we
enter that now?

00:54:12.690 --> 00:54:14.500 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, so now you have
to type in one times

00:54:14.500 --> 00:54:15.010 align:middle line:90%
whatever-- equals--

00:54:15.010 --> 00:54:16.570 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: You've got
to type in what this is.

00:54:16.570 --> 00:54:18.430 align:middle line:84%
So and this is actually
getting at another

00:54:18.430 --> 00:54:19.680 align:middle line:90%
representation.

00:54:19.680 --> 00:54:23.590 align:middle line:90%


00:54:23.590 --> 00:54:24.820 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Eight plus nine.

00:54:24.820 --> 00:54:27.468 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Eight plus
nine is 17.

00:54:27.468 --> 00:54:28.884 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: How, here you
don't have to actually

00:54:28.884 --> 00:54:30.300 align:middle line:90%
remember the [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:34.920 align:middle line:90%


00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:36.170 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Just divide
it by six.

00:54:36.170 --> 00:54:50.588 align:middle line:90%


00:54:50.588 --> 00:54:51.838 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:54:51.838 --> 00:54:55.100 align:middle line:90%


00:54:55.100 --> 00:54:56.550 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: It's puzzle mode.

00:54:56.550 --> 00:54:58.686 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And by the
way, it could take

00:54:58.686 --> 00:55:00.001 align:middle line:90%
it in either order.

00:55:00.001 --> 00:55:04.438 align:middle line:90%


00:55:04.438 --> 00:55:05.917 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I agree.

00:55:05.917 --> 00:55:07.167 align:middle line:90%
Equals [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:55:07.167 --> 00:55:10.354 align:middle line:90%


00:55:10.354 --> 00:55:12.819 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I think this
level's only addition

00:55:12.819 --> 00:55:14.069 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:55:14.069 --> 00:55:17.290 align:middle line:90%


00:55:17.290 --> 00:55:18.540 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:55:18.540 --> 00:55:21.750 align:middle line:90%


00:55:21.750 --> 00:55:25.470 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Or we could
do 10 minus two.

00:55:25.470 --> 00:55:26.090 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Anyway.

00:55:26.090 --> 00:55:27.730 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:55:27.730 --> 00:55:29.630 align:middle line:90%
you were doing algebra.

00:55:29.630 --> 00:55:31.160 align:middle line:84%
When you were solving
for those numbers,

00:55:31.160 --> 00:55:32.456 align:middle line:90%
you were doing algebra.

00:55:32.456 --> 00:55:35.916 align:middle line:84%
You were really thinking about
x and the structure of the

00:55:35.916 --> 00:55:39.800 align:middle line:84%
math and the numerical
quality of it.

00:55:39.800 --> 00:55:43.109 align:middle line:84%
This is simpler, but it's still
getting at the fact that

00:55:43.109 --> 00:55:45.001 align:middle line:84%
there are multiple ways of
representing numbers

00:55:45.001 --> 00:55:45.474 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:55:45.474 --> 00:55:46.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Is there
any mechanic to

00:55:46.970 --> 00:55:47.770 align:middle line:90%
prevent brute force?

00:55:47.770 --> 00:55:50.850 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes, those
lights go out.

00:55:50.850 --> 00:55:53.500 align:middle line:84%
But, you do earn points along
the way in the game if you

00:55:53.500 --> 00:55:54.970 align:middle line:90%
make some progress.

00:55:54.970 --> 00:55:58.715 align:middle line:84%
And so, none of these puzzles
are brick walls.

00:55:58.715 --> 00:55:59.940 align:middle line:84%
Because you can always play
multiple puzzles at once.

00:55:59.940 --> 00:56:03.750 align:middle line:84%
You can leave this puzzle,
go to another puzzle.

00:56:03.750 --> 00:56:06.140 align:middle line:84%
And, if you make any progress
at all, you're accumulating

00:56:06.140 --> 00:56:07.014 align:middle line:84%
points for the end
of the game.

00:56:07.014 --> 00:56:08.762 align:middle line:84%
You could actually get to the
end of the game story without

00:56:08.762 --> 00:56:09.900 align:middle line:90%
having solved any puzzles.

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:11.490 align:middle line:90%
Takes a long time.

00:56:11.490 --> 00:56:13.280 align:middle line:84%
The rewards are there
for doing well.

00:56:13.280 --> 00:56:16.255 align:middle line:84%
And there are rewards for doing
well, but there isn't

00:56:16.255 --> 00:56:18.195 align:middle line:90%
punishment for not doing well.

00:56:18.195 --> 00:56:21.110 align:middle line:90%
At least in sort of a--

00:56:21.110 --> 00:56:24.347 align:middle line:84%
we don't say you can't,
we don't make you

00:56:24.347 --> 00:56:25.305 align:middle line:90%
stop in your tracks.

00:56:25.305 --> 00:56:26.742 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
you can do it again.

00:56:26.742 --> 00:56:27.221 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes,
that's right.

00:56:27.221 --> 00:56:28.025 align:middle line:84%
You can do it again
instantaneously.

00:56:28.025 --> 00:56:32.760 align:middle line:84%
And there is no cost in the
game to having failed

00:56:32.760 --> 00:56:34.770 align:middle line:90%
repeatedly.

00:56:34.770 --> 00:56:37.845 align:middle line:84%
If you eventually solve it, you
end up with the same score

00:56:37.845 --> 00:56:42.045 align:middle line:84%
whether you solved it on the
20th time or the first time.

00:56:42.045 --> 00:56:43.590 align:middle line:84%
That's really what problem
solving is about.

00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:45.800 align:middle line:84%
It's not about being
the fastest.

00:56:45.800 --> 00:56:49.600 align:middle line:84%
It's just about learning
how to solve things.

00:56:49.600 --> 00:56:52.435 align:middle line:84%
Speed is overrated
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:56:52.435 --> 00:56:54.742 align:middle line:84%
And in math it's
very overrated.

00:56:54.742 --> 00:56:56.598 align:middle line:90%
Kids [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:56:56.598 --> 00:56:59.640 align:middle line:90%
calculations in their head.

00:56:59.640 --> 00:57:00.370 align:middle line:90%
It's just more of this.

00:57:00.370 --> 00:57:02.050 align:middle line:90%
And the entire level's--

00:57:02.050 --> 00:57:04.250 align:middle line:84%
we came up with representations
of

00:57:04.250 --> 00:57:08.340 align:middle line:84%
subtraction, which is that you
have a large number, you have

00:57:08.340 --> 00:57:10.958 align:middle line:84%
10 jars and five of
them are empty.

00:57:10.958 --> 00:57:14.227 align:middle line:90%
So it's 10 minus five.

00:57:14.227 --> 00:57:14.700 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:57:14.700 --> 00:57:16.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: How is division?

00:57:16.890 --> 00:57:19.542 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are they
10 rows of jars?

00:57:19.542 --> 00:57:20.940 align:middle line:90%
And the rows are empty?

00:57:20.940 --> 00:57:22.995 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, I
think it's the same--

00:57:22.995 --> 00:57:24.975 align:middle line:90%
I think that's it.

00:57:24.975 --> 00:57:27.950 align:middle line:90%
I think that's the same.

00:57:27.950 --> 00:57:32.914 align:middle line:84%
It's in a grid pattern with
some [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

00:57:32.914 --> 00:57:36.395 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And I guess there's
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:57:36.395 --> 00:57:41.130 align:middle line:84%
but do you have any algebra
as such in this format?

00:57:41.130 --> 00:57:41.780 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: No.

00:57:41.780 --> 00:57:44.040 align:middle line:84%
But what we encourage
is that--

00:57:44.040 --> 00:57:45.700 align:middle line:84%
so what comes with this is
material for teachers to

00:57:45.700 --> 00:57:47.394 align:middle line:90%
relate this to the curriculum.

00:57:47.394 --> 00:57:50.787 align:middle line:84%
And what we really want to
happen is that kids have

00:57:50.787 --> 00:57:52.388 align:middle line:84%
played the game before
they get the subject.

00:57:52.388 --> 00:57:55.184 align:middle line:84%
And when the teacher introduces
symbol systems in

00:57:55.184 --> 00:57:58.770 align:middle line:84%
base six or whatever relates
to this, instead of saying,

00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:01.100 align:middle line:84%
all right, we're starting a
new unit, you again know

00:58:01.100 --> 00:58:04.090 align:middle line:84%
nothing, you're starting again
at square one and have to

00:58:04.090 --> 00:58:05.990 align:middle line:84%
start all over again
with something new.

00:58:05.990 --> 00:58:08.690 align:middle line:84%
Instead, the teacher comes in
and says, I want to introduce

00:58:08.690 --> 00:58:11.580 align:middle line:84%
a new concept, but it's one
that you've really already

00:58:11.580 --> 00:58:13.550 align:middle line:90%
begun to master.

00:58:13.550 --> 00:58:16.740 align:middle line:90%
And it's called bases.

00:58:16.740 --> 00:58:18.290 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:58:18.290 --> 00:58:19.443 align:middle line:90%
so how did you solve this?

00:58:19.443 --> 00:58:22.680 align:middle line:84%
What was going on
in this game?

00:58:22.680 --> 00:58:25.470 align:middle line:84%
And they have a conversation
about what they already know,

00:58:25.470 --> 00:58:29.082 align:middle line:90%
as a way of leveraging into--

00:58:29.082 --> 00:58:33.276 align:middle line:84%
I mean, things that I
arguably don't know.

00:58:33.276 --> 00:58:36.080 align:middle line:84%
Again, I would argue that
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:58:36.080 --> 00:58:37.240 align:middle line:90%
you have things in cartons.

00:58:37.240 --> 00:58:42.420 align:middle line:84%
So a carton of six jars plus
some left over would be--

00:58:42.420 --> 00:58:45.170 align:middle line:84%
a carton of six jars plus three
left over would be one,

00:58:45.170 --> 00:58:47.000 align:middle line:90%
three, basically.

00:58:47.000 --> 00:58:50.340 align:middle line:90%
So anyway.

00:58:50.340 --> 00:58:53.190 align:middle line:84%
The point is that the kids begin
to build this robust

00:58:53.190 --> 00:58:54.500 align:middle line:90%
conceptual understanding.

00:58:54.500 --> 00:58:57.327 align:middle line:84%
And hopefully there's a
playfulness so that when the

00:58:57.327 --> 00:59:00.990 align:middle line:84%
teacher talks about it, that
some of them may actually feel

00:59:00.990 --> 00:59:01.320 align:middle line:90%
good about it.

00:59:01.320 --> 00:59:05.265 align:middle line:84%
Instead of, here's one more
reading --you'll never be a

00:59:05.265 --> 00:59:06.660 align:middle line:90%
mathematician.

00:59:06.660 --> 00:59:09.170 align:middle line:84%
I think math education is
largely designed to gate

00:59:09.170 --> 00:59:12.390 align:middle line:84%
everybody so that at the
end of high school,

00:59:12.390 --> 00:59:13.984 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] population
is good at math.

00:59:13.984 --> 00:59:17.721 align:middle line:84%
Which means that we're taking
12 years teaching 93% of the

00:59:17.721 --> 00:59:19.017 align:middle line:84%
population that's no good at
math and they should stop

00:59:19.017 --> 00:59:19.266 align:middle line:90%
doing that.

00:59:19.266 --> 00:59:22.382 align:middle line:84%
I don't know why we'd waste 12
years if you could just tell

00:59:22.382 --> 00:59:23.968 align:middle line:90%
them to go to kindergarten.

00:59:23.968 --> 00:59:26.060 align:middle line:84%
But that's what what
we end up doing.

00:59:26.060 --> 00:59:27.310 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, I think that's true
of every subject.

00:59:27.310 --> 00:59:30.680 align:middle line:90%


00:59:30.680 --> 00:59:31.830 align:middle line:90%
I'd like to turn it around.

00:59:31.830 --> 00:59:33.934 align:middle line:84%
Everyone says there's things
about math I can do.

00:59:33.934 --> 00:59:36.339 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

00:59:36.339 --> 00:59:42.780 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Can you tell us a
little bit more about how that

00:59:42.780 --> 00:59:43.910 align:middle line:90%
process went?

00:59:43.910 --> 00:59:44.880 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Sure.

00:59:44.880 --> 00:59:47.660 align:middle line:84%
And I also wanted to provide a
little extra nuance to one of

00:59:47.660 --> 00:59:51.540 align:middle line:84%
the questions about what if you
just brute-force your way

00:59:51.540 --> 00:59:51.780 align:middle line:90%
through it.

00:59:51.780 --> 00:59:56.878 align:middle line:84%
In the higher levels, it won't
tell you when you put a symbol

00:59:56.878 --> 00:59:58.324 align:middle line:84%
in a slot, whether it's
correct or not.

00:59:58.324 --> 00:59:59.770 align:middle line:90%
So--

00:59:59.770 --> 01:00:02.180 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Which actually,
you could learn something

01:00:02.180 --> 01:00:02.662 align:middle line:90%
interesting.

01:00:02.662 --> 01:00:05.554 align:middle line:84%
Because you could start doing
something and [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:00:05.554 --> 01:00:07.480 align:middle line:84%
something that doesn't add
up, that's actually new.

01:00:07.480 --> 01:00:10.340 align:middle line:90%
You're learning something.

01:00:10.340 --> 01:00:14.720 align:middle line:84%
So, there's 10 puzzles
or-- how many?

01:00:14.720 --> 01:00:15.045 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Nine.

01:00:15.045 --> 01:00:22.980 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Nine puzzles, and we
came up with nine plus x, and

01:00:22.980 --> 01:00:29.390 align:middle line:84%
some of that was slated for
school admission and

01:00:29.390 --> 01:00:31.350 align:middle line:90%
we never got there.

01:00:31.350 --> 01:00:36.413 align:middle line:84%
And some of that x was puzzles
or activities that we decided

01:00:36.413 --> 01:00:38.210 align:middle line:84%
were not as compelling
as the others.

01:00:38.210 --> 01:00:41.528 align:middle line:84%
In our process for that, we
did a hybrid approach with

01:00:41.528 --> 01:00:43.750 align:middle line:84%
paper and computer
prototyping.

01:00:43.750 --> 01:00:48.076 align:middle line:84%
In general the rule is, if there
can be paper prototypes,

01:00:48.076 --> 01:00:48.801 align:middle line:90%
paper prototype it.

01:00:48.801 --> 01:00:49.767 align:middle line:90%
Because it's so much faster.

01:00:49.767 --> 01:00:53.148 align:middle line:84%
But there are certain kinds of
activities that really can't.

01:00:53.148 --> 01:00:56.529 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, this one is
perfect for paper prototyping.

01:00:56.529 --> 01:00:58.501 align:middle line:84%
You just take out a
piece of paper and

01:00:58.501 --> 01:00:59.745 align:middle line:90%
a pen and you create--

01:00:59.745 --> 01:01:02.756 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you have to create some
of these equations so

01:01:02.756 --> 01:01:05.310 align:middle line:84%
that you then have a system
of equations to solve.

01:01:05.310 --> 01:01:08.500 align:middle line:84%
But once you've created some of
those is, as would need to

01:01:08.500 --> 01:01:12.710 align:middle line:84%
be done whether it's a paper or
a computer prototype, then

01:01:12.710 --> 01:01:15.137 align:middle line:84%
it's trivial to put it in front
of someone with paper.

01:01:15.137 --> 01:01:19.220 align:middle line:84%
So this is an example of
something where we would, for

01:01:19.220 --> 01:01:23.960 align:middle line:84%
the first round, test internally
with just symbols

01:01:23.960 --> 01:01:25.030 align:middle line:90%
and numbers.

01:01:25.030 --> 01:01:28.175 align:middle line:84%
And then probably
we would do--

01:01:28.175 --> 01:01:31.110 align:middle line:84%
we might even test that version
with people in our

01:01:31.110 --> 01:01:31.950 align:middle line:90%
target audience.

01:01:31.950 --> 01:01:35.180 align:middle line:84%
But probably we would try to
do some rudimentary art

01:01:35.180 --> 01:01:38.530 align:middle line:84%
treatment before we put it in
front of people, just so

01:01:38.530 --> 01:01:40.785 align:middle line:84%
there's a little bit of context
and it looks a little

01:01:40.785 --> 01:01:41.990 align:middle line:90%
bit more like a game.

01:01:41.990 --> 01:01:43.450 align:middle line:90%
And then--

01:01:43.450 --> 01:01:44.370 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So
that we try to--

01:01:44.370 --> 01:01:48.483 align:middle line:84%
if there's story, this one has
less story about it, but if

01:01:48.483 --> 01:01:51.205 align:middle line:84%
there's story, as there are in
some puzzles, we actually try

01:01:51.205 --> 01:01:51.635 align:middle line:90%
to act it out.

01:01:51.635 --> 01:01:52.922 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:01:52.922 --> 01:01:54.410 align:middle line:90%
resistance to doing it.

01:01:54.410 --> 01:01:56.890 align:middle line:90%
So act out the character.

01:01:56.890 --> 01:01:59.635 align:middle line:84%
You're the computer, when
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:01:59.635 --> 01:02:01.806 align:middle line:84%
and so even if it's just a
character giving a response to

01:02:01.806 --> 01:02:03.391 align:middle line:84%
something that the
player does.

01:02:03.391 --> 01:02:06.255 align:middle line:84%
You try to do it in character,
you try to get a sense of

01:02:06.255 --> 01:02:08.870 align:middle line:84%
what-- because I think, even
in that kind of rudimentary

01:02:08.870 --> 01:02:12.050 align:middle line:84%
form you're engaging the
player's imagination so you

01:02:12.050 --> 01:02:14.930 align:middle line:84%
know whether or not they're
interested in this character

01:02:14.930 --> 01:02:17.355 align:middle line:90%
or the idea [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:02:17.355 --> 01:02:20.265 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Because I do
master [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:02:20.265 --> 01:02:23.660 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And like a
dungeon master you need to

01:02:23.660 --> 01:02:26.610 align:middle line:84%
prepare, because it's hard to
be the computer without also

01:02:26.610 --> 01:02:29.635 align:middle line:84%
being the bottleneck
if you're a human.

01:02:29.635 --> 01:02:33.660 align:middle line:84%
And in fact we've done some
games, not represented here,

01:02:33.660 --> 01:02:36.480 align:middle line:84%
we've done some games that are
real time, that if you want to

01:02:36.480 --> 01:02:40.508 align:middle line:84%
prototype with people, then
especially on paper, and you

01:02:40.508 --> 01:02:42.640 align:middle line:84%
want to keep things moving,
then you need to have more

01:02:42.640 --> 01:02:45.278 align:middle line:84%
than one person be the computer,
because there's just

01:02:45.278 --> 01:02:48.710 align:middle line:84%
too much to track for one
person to do it all.

01:02:48.710 --> 01:02:49.570 align:middle line:90%
Or if you want to do--

01:02:49.570 --> 01:02:53.566 align:middle line:84%
sometimes we do hybrids, where
we'll do a computer prototype

01:02:53.566 --> 01:02:56.440 align:middle line:84%
but there's aspects of the game
that are not represented

01:02:56.440 --> 01:02:58.070 align:middle line:84%
through the interface or that
logically we have a model, so

01:02:58.070 --> 01:03:02.180 align:middle line:84%
we'll do players
directing human

01:03:02.180 --> 01:03:06.190 align:middle line:90%
interactions with the computer.

01:03:06.190 --> 01:03:09.092 align:middle line:84%
Not players, designers,
influencing those

01:03:09.092 --> 01:03:10.342 align:middle line:90%
interactions.

01:03:10.342 --> 01:03:12.459 align:middle line:90%


01:03:12.459 --> 01:03:16.905 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, this is a game that
moved pretty much all the way

01:03:16.905 --> 01:03:20.175 align:middle line:84%
to final design on paper, and
then went straight to

01:03:20.175 --> 01:03:20.730 align:middle line:90%
implementation.

01:03:20.730 --> 01:03:25.436 align:middle line:84%
Some of the other games-- for
instance, there's a game--

01:03:25.436 --> 01:03:28.412 align:middle line:84%
do you want to show
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:03:28.412 --> 01:03:29.900 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE],
yeah.

01:03:29.900 --> 01:03:33.868 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: That's an example
of a game that's a

01:03:33.868 --> 01:03:37.990 align:middle line:84%
little bit harder to think about
playing just on paper.

01:03:37.990 --> 01:03:38.520 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
we

01:03:38.520 --> 01:03:39.567 align:middle line:90%
implemented this early.

01:03:39.567 --> 01:03:41.058 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, we did.

01:03:41.058 --> 01:03:48.520 align:middle line:90%


01:03:48.520 --> 01:03:54.740 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So this is a game
about modular arithmetic

01:03:54.740 --> 01:03:59.690 align:middle line:84%
and common multiples and
that sort of thing.

01:03:59.690 --> 01:04:01.230 align:middle line:84%
So it's very hard to
see on the screen.

01:04:01.230 --> 01:04:03.403 align:middle line:84%
In the upper left there
is an avatar.

01:04:03.403 --> 01:04:05.318 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: That's you.

01:04:05.318 --> 01:04:06.568 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:04:06.568 --> 01:04:08.570 align:middle line:90%


01:04:08.570 --> 01:04:11.320 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And you know from
the story that your goal

01:04:11.320 --> 01:04:14.052 align:middle line:84%
is to get to the golden
eggs and avoid the

01:04:14.052 --> 01:04:14.534 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:04:14.534 --> 01:04:15.980 align:middle line:90%
That much you know.

01:04:15.980 --> 01:04:21.790 align:middle line:84%
And that you're in a factory
where they make stuff.

01:04:21.790 --> 01:04:24.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you move
your avatar?

01:04:24.100 --> 01:04:25.350 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:04:25.350 --> 01:04:40.440 align:middle line:90%


01:04:40.440 --> 01:04:41.690 align:middle line:90%
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:04:41.690 --> 01:04:50.390 align:middle line:90%


01:04:50.390 --> 01:04:53.770 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: There is one
flaw, by the way, in the

01:04:53.770 --> 01:04:56.420 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:04:56.420 --> 01:04:58.426 align:middle line:84%
interface, because people don't
realize they have to

01:04:58.426 --> 01:05:00.580 align:middle line:84%
click to get their guy
onto that thing.

01:05:00.580 --> 01:05:04.190 align:middle line:84%
And it was supposed to start
with the [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:05:04.190 --> 01:05:07.470 align:middle line:84%
right where he was, that people
would [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:05:07.470 --> 01:05:07.623 align:middle line:90%
click.

01:05:07.623 --> 01:05:10.410 align:middle line:84%
Because now by-- what
should I try now.

01:05:10.410 --> 01:05:10.906 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Four.

01:05:10.906 --> 01:05:12.394 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: We're
going to go--

01:05:12.394 --> 01:05:16.362 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Four or nine.

01:05:16.362 --> 01:05:18.346 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Six.

01:05:18.346 --> 01:05:18.842 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Four.

01:05:18.842 --> 01:05:19.834 align:middle line:90%
Four.

01:05:19.834 --> 01:05:20.826 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Six.

01:05:20.826 --> 01:05:23.306 align:middle line:84%
Then you can let him get
the [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:05:23.306 --> 01:05:25.290 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I'll
go for six.

01:05:25.290 --> 01:05:29.490 align:middle line:90%


01:05:29.490 --> 01:05:31.280 align:middle line:90%
Bam.

01:05:31.280 --> 01:05:32.400 align:middle line:90%
Four is a subject that--

01:05:32.400 --> 01:05:33.880 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Oh, can we
move [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:05:33.880 --> 01:05:34.870 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: But you'll notice
that that's all in

01:05:34.870 --> 01:05:35.510 align:middle line:90%
different amounts.

01:05:35.510 --> 01:05:38.930 align:middle line:84%
And so it's really that
modular arithmetic.

01:05:38.930 --> 01:05:43.480 align:middle line:84%
So 12 will take me once around
on one of them, but

01:05:43.480 --> 01:05:45.736 align:middle line:84%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]
on another one.

01:05:45.736 --> 01:05:48.582 align:middle line:84%
And you start doing simultaneous
equations to

01:05:48.582 --> 01:05:50.270 align:middle line:90%
solve this.

01:05:50.270 --> 01:05:51.960 align:middle line:90%
This level's hard.

01:05:51.960 --> 01:05:53.870 align:middle line:84%
I think maybe the hardest puzzle
we designed in the

01:05:53.870 --> 01:05:57.870 align:middle line:84%
whole game is level three of
this, where every step you've

01:05:57.870 --> 01:06:01.460 align:middle line:90%
got to align two bridges.

01:06:01.460 --> 01:06:02.850 align:middle line:90%
It's serious work.

01:06:02.850 --> 01:06:05.602 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Are you trying
to avoid that monster?

01:06:05.602 --> 01:06:06.250 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, you've
got to avoid the monster.

01:06:06.250 --> 01:06:06.690 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Which
isn't always hard.

01:06:06.690 --> 01:06:09.890 align:middle line:84%
But you have to keep in mind,
sometimes it needs, instead of

01:06:09.890 --> 01:06:12.630 align:middle line:84%
going around once, particularly
one on the nine

01:06:12.630 --> 01:06:20.030 align:middle line:84%
thing, maybe you go 10 and
he'll always go on

01:06:20.030 --> 01:06:20.665 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:06:20.665 --> 01:06:22.214 align:middle line:84%
shows up, to make
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:06:22.214 --> 01:06:23.660 align:middle line:90%
to show up and [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:06:23.660 --> 01:06:26.070 align:middle line:84%
So this is really the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:06:26.070 --> 01:06:27.034 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: As of what?

01:06:27.034 --> 01:06:28.284 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

01:06:28.284 --> 01:06:30.408 align:middle line:90%


01:06:30.408 --> 01:06:31.960 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Anyway, again
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:06:31.960 --> 01:06:34.100 align:middle line:84%
you can say, hey,
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:06:34.100 --> 01:06:35.530 align:middle line:90%
math, school math at all.

01:06:35.530 --> 01:06:39.230 align:middle line:84%
There's no school math
in this game.

01:06:39.230 --> 01:06:40.740 align:middle line:84%
But I think there are
really interesting

01:06:40.740 --> 01:06:43.030 align:middle line:90%
things to think about.

01:06:43.030 --> 01:06:43.980 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:06:43.980 --> 01:06:45.550 align:middle line:90%
said with that character.

01:06:45.550 --> 01:06:46.870 align:middle line:90%
If he walks or not.

01:06:46.870 --> 01:06:49.445 align:middle line:90%
I think we [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:06:49.445 --> 01:06:49.780 align:middle line:90%
anyway.

01:06:49.780 --> 01:06:51.680 align:middle line:84%
But you have to keep
in mind, there are

01:06:51.680 --> 01:06:52.160 align:middle line:90%
two forms of learning.

01:06:52.160 --> 01:06:54.470 align:middle line:84%
One is learning the game,
how to play the game.

01:06:54.470 --> 01:06:56.940 align:middle line:84%
And the other one is learning--
there's a problem

01:06:56.940 --> 01:06:58.580 align:middle line:84%
solving, you've got a
puzzle in the game.

01:06:58.580 --> 01:07:02.010 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's sometimes like
special education games.

01:07:02.010 --> 01:07:04.440 align:middle line:90%
It's so easy to mix this.

01:07:04.440 --> 01:07:07.190 align:middle line:84%
Because sometimes you make the
coolest educational game and

01:07:07.190 --> 01:07:08.260 align:middle line:90%
it's really interesting.

01:07:08.260 --> 01:07:10.000 align:middle line:90%
But [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:07:10.000 --> 01:07:13.180 align:middle line:84%
character wouldn't move if you
don't press the mouse button.

01:07:13.180 --> 01:07:16.885 align:middle line:84%
So he would always stand there
in the beginning and he would

01:07:16.885 --> 01:07:17.650 align:middle line:90%
get attacked.

01:07:17.650 --> 01:07:20.770 align:middle line:84%
And I think the mixture of
both forms of learning is

01:07:20.770 --> 01:07:23.030 align:middle line:84%
quite interesting to keep in
mind when you're prototyping.

01:07:23.030 --> 01:07:25.310 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, there's
two answers to that.

01:07:25.310 --> 01:07:27.000 align:middle line:90%
One is, I never think.

01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:29.880 align:middle line:84%
just because we want people to
think hard, I don't think the

01:07:29.880 --> 01:07:31.080 align:middle line:84%
interface should be the
thing that they're

01:07:31.080 --> 01:07:31.560 align:middle line:90%
thinking hard about.

01:07:31.560 --> 01:07:35.350 align:middle line:84%
It's always tempting to say,
oh, that's a really

01:07:35.350 --> 01:07:36.480 align:middle line:90%
hard thing to solve.

01:07:36.480 --> 01:07:38.240 align:middle line:90%
How do I make him jump?

01:07:38.240 --> 01:07:42.364 align:middle line:84%
But no, I think generally it
should not be hard to figure

01:07:42.364 --> 01:07:42.830 align:middle line:90%
out how to make him jump.

01:07:42.830 --> 01:07:46.560 align:middle line:84%
Or walk left or right, or
how to open a door.

01:07:46.560 --> 01:07:50.040 align:middle line:84%
Unless that's a puzzle, and it's
easy to delude yourself

01:07:50.040 --> 01:07:51.552 align:middle line:84%
that by making the
interface hard...

01:07:51.552 --> 01:07:54.793 align:middle line:84%
On the other hand, there is this
other challenge, which is

01:07:54.793 --> 01:07:56.800 align:middle line:84%
that kids do have different
degrees of

01:07:56.800 --> 01:07:58.125 align:middle line:90%
literacy around games.

01:07:58.125 --> 01:08:00.722 align:middle line:84%
And it's hard to figure out
where the sweet spot is.

01:08:00.722 --> 01:08:02.935 align:middle line:84%
That's one I don't have
an easy answer for.

01:08:02.935 --> 01:08:04.570 align:middle line:84%
Because some large percentage
of the audience naturally

01:08:04.570 --> 01:08:07.572 align:middle line:90%
knows, try and get to the end.

01:08:07.572 --> 01:08:09.130 align:middle line:90%
Knows what winning means.

01:08:09.130 --> 01:08:10.870 align:middle line:84%
But there's some
kids who don't.

01:08:10.870 --> 01:08:13.330 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, if you
make the game, if you bend

01:08:13.330 --> 01:08:15.870 align:middle line:90%
over backwards--

01:08:15.870 --> 01:08:19.819 align:middle line:84%
I watched my mother play this,
and it was horrible.

01:08:19.819 --> 01:08:24.484 align:middle line:84%
Because she just was totally
clueless about where to go.

01:08:24.484 --> 01:08:27.690 align:middle line:84%
But I think if I try to set
up the game to work for my

01:08:27.690 --> 01:08:31.660 align:middle line:84%
mother, you can only work with
all this explicit instruction.

01:08:31.660 --> 01:08:36.330 align:middle line:84%
So hopefully with kids it's
less of an issue.

01:08:36.330 --> 01:08:38.370 align:middle line:84%
There's more, I think, uniform
game literacy.

01:08:38.370 --> 01:08:39.419 align:middle line:90%
But it's always a challenge.

01:08:39.419 --> 01:08:41.000 align:middle line:90%
No game appeals to everybody.

01:08:41.000 --> 01:08:43.880 align:middle line:90%
And you do want--

01:08:43.880 --> 01:08:45.365 align:middle line:84%
I don't think those of us who
make learning games have a

01:08:45.365 --> 01:08:46.399 align:middle line:90%
good answer to that.

01:08:46.399 --> 01:08:48.390 align:middle line:84%
I don't think we can ever make
a game that every kid would

01:08:48.390 --> 01:08:52.397 align:middle line:90%
play with equal--

01:08:52.397 --> 01:08:55.430 align:middle line:84%
we should never claim
that we're going

01:08:55.430 --> 01:08:59.103 align:middle line:90%
to get 100% on that.

01:08:59.103 --> 01:09:02.825 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: One thing that I know
that you've noticed is

01:09:02.825 --> 01:09:08.044 align:middle line:84%
the amount of tolerance that
kids and adults have for

01:09:08.044 --> 01:09:10.580 align:middle line:90%
frustration, when--

01:09:10.580 --> 01:09:14.200 align:middle line:84%
I've seen kids say,
I hate this game.

01:09:14.200 --> 01:09:20.232 align:middle line:84%
This game is too hard and
they'll just keep going at it.

01:09:20.232 --> 01:09:24.624 align:middle line:84%
Whereas the adults have this
tendency to get really

01:09:24.624 --> 01:09:27.552 align:middle line:84%
embarrassed when they can't
solve it first time.

01:09:27.552 --> 01:09:30.689 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: It happens
when you're 26.

01:09:30.689 --> 01:09:33.060 align:middle line:84%
You suddenly sit down and
say, well, I'm not

01:09:33.060 --> 01:09:34.080 align:middle line:90%
very good at this.

01:09:34.080 --> 01:09:38.223 align:middle line:84%
You may be a gamer all your
life, but it's having another

01:09:38.223 --> 01:09:40.211 align:middle line:90%
adult watching you.

01:09:40.211 --> 01:09:43.855 align:middle line:84%
There's some point or another
where you suddenly say, I'm

01:09:43.855 --> 01:09:44.187 align:middle line:90%
not good at this.

01:09:44.187 --> 01:09:46.175 align:middle line:90%
I don't know why it happens.

01:09:46.175 --> 01:09:48.660 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:09:48.660 --> 01:09:49.910 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:09:49.910 --> 01:09:53.630 align:middle line:90%


01:09:53.630 --> 01:09:55.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you do
negative numbers?

01:09:55.650 --> 01:09:56.983 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Not
in this one, no.

01:09:56.983 --> 01:09:59.298 align:middle line:90%
That's a good idea, though.

01:09:59.298 --> 01:10:00.690 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:10:00.690 --> 01:10:02.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: In much of the
arithmetic you could always do

01:10:02.090 --> 01:10:04.312 align:middle line:84%
it as put in a larger number
and [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:10:04.312 --> 01:10:05.764 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: That's right.

01:10:05.764 --> 01:10:08.184 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I have a question
for [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:10:08.184 --> 01:10:11.572 align:middle line:84%
Because in some of them we were
hearing from you guys

01:10:11.572 --> 01:10:15.626 align:middle line:84%
about coming, more or less from
a game design angle into

01:10:15.626 --> 01:10:19.618 align:middle line:84%
designing games as a
learning purpose.

01:10:19.618 --> 01:10:21.614 align:middle line:90%
And you kind of [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:10:21.614 --> 01:10:22.612 align:middle line:90%
the other way around.

01:10:22.612 --> 01:10:24.608 align:middle line:84%
You came in from the
[UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:10:24.608 --> 01:10:28.766 align:middle line:84%
Are there things to keep in
mind when you're working

01:10:28.766 --> 01:10:31.095 align:middle line:84%
across disciplines, that
game designers know?

01:10:31.095 --> 01:10:34.610 align:middle line:90%


01:10:34.610 --> 01:10:38.450 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So, I think one
more difference that I still

01:10:38.450 --> 01:10:42.330 align:middle line:84%
have, or see between the way I
approach games and the way

01:10:42.330 --> 01:10:46.170 align:middle line:84%
educational games approach games
design, I'm more focused

01:10:46.170 --> 01:10:49.030 align:middle line:84%
on learning as an experience,
more in abstract form.

01:10:49.030 --> 01:10:51.030 align:middle line:84%
That doesn't mean that I
wouldn't be invested in that,

01:10:51.030 --> 01:10:54.285 align:middle line:84%
but that's not what I've been
working on yet here.

01:10:54.285 --> 01:10:56.530 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, after
that it was--

01:10:56.530 --> 01:11:00.780 align:middle line:84%
so the research question was
how, how can players overcome

01:11:00.780 --> 01:11:04.550 align:middle line:90%
expectations that are wrong.

01:11:04.550 --> 01:11:09.620 align:middle line:84%
And so the idea was not to use
educational stuff like numbers

01:11:09.620 --> 01:11:14.130 align:middle line:84%
or whatever, but more, can we
develop patterns in games that

01:11:14.130 --> 01:11:17.700 align:middle line:84%
players who realize very
quickly, and are they ready to

01:11:17.700 --> 01:11:20.360 align:middle line:84%
give them up if they realize
that they think what they've

01:11:20.360 --> 01:11:21.600 align:middle line:90%
seen is wrong.

01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:24.856 align:middle line:84%
So, for me it was very
interesting because I come at

01:11:24.856 --> 01:11:26.890 align:middle line:90%
an abstract theory inside.

01:11:26.890 --> 01:11:29.210 align:middle line:84%
And so we were developing
prototypes.

01:11:29.210 --> 01:11:31.450 align:middle line:84%
And it's very interesting when
you come from the abstract

01:11:31.450 --> 01:11:33.910 align:middle line:90%
idea to prototypes.

01:11:33.910 --> 01:11:37.520 align:middle line:84%
But paper prototyping is perfect
because it really made

01:11:37.520 --> 01:11:40.150 align:middle line:84%
me as a scientist come
to the ground.

01:11:40.150 --> 01:11:42.150 align:middle line:84%
Because of course I can read
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] and all these

01:11:42.150 --> 01:11:45.700 align:middle line:84%
people and say, wow, experiences
this and that.

01:11:45.700 --> 01:11:48.720 align:middle line:84%
And then you suddenly have pen
and paper and sit there and

01:11:48.720 --> 01:11:54.780 align:middle line:84%
think about a game that, is it
a metaphor for your theory.

01:11:54.780 --> 01:12:00.230 align:middle line:84%
And so I think my takeaway was
that paper prototyping was

01:12:00.230 --> 01:12:02.020 align:middle line:90%
very healthy for me.

01:12:02.020 --> 01:12:04.790 align:middle line:84%
And with the team to come down
to the fact, what is really

01:12:04.790 --> 01:12:06.180 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE] in the game.

01:12:06.180 --> 01:12:06.670 align:middle line:90%
What can we really--

01:12:06.670 --> 01:12:09.890 align:middle line:84%
I think at the beginning I was
a bit inexperienced in

01:12:09.890 --> 01:12:11.610 align:middle line:90%
prototyping.

01:12:11.610 --> 01:12:13.080 align:middle line:90%
Wow, we can all do that later.

01:12:13.080 --> 01:12:16.370 align:middle line:84%
We can do the cool stuff when
we get to programming.

01:12:16.370 --> 01:12:18.270 align:middle line:84%
But then I realized,
no, we can't.

01:12:18.270 --> 01:12:21.960 align:middle line:84%
We cannot do it as a paper
prototype, how should we do it

01:12:21.960 --> 01:12:23.100 align:middle line:90%
in programming.

01:12:23.100 --> 01:12:25.290 align:middle line:90%
But what is changing?

01:12:25.290 --> 01:12:29.390 align:middle line:84%
And I think for us it was
very interesting.

01:12:29.390 --> 01:12:32.910 align:middle line:84%
So I think one experience we
had is, we had them develop

01:12:32.910 --> 01:12:34.460 align:middle line:90%
different prototypes.

01:12:34.460 --> 01:12:37.390 align:middle line:84%
And so I think that was healthy
for us not to make one

01:12:37.390 --> 01:12:40.250 align:middle line:84%
paper prototype but then think
how we can do it better.

01:12:40.250 --> 01:12:42.770 align:middle line:90%
But that's what [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:12:42.770 --> 01:12:45.650 align:middle line:90%
a lot of prototypes.

01:12:45.650 --> 01:12:46.895 align:middle line:90%
And to stay open.

01:12:46.895 --> 01:12:50.280 align:middle line:84%
And even if you spend three or
four days on a prototype, if

01:12:50.280 --> 01:12:52.440 align:middle line:84%
it doesn't work with others
the moment you show them.

01:12:52.440 --> 01:12:54.040 align:middle line:84%
And they go like, what,
these are the rules.

01:12:54.040 --> 01:12:55.260 align:middle line:90%
Are you sure?

01:12:55.260 --> 01:12:57.132 align:middle line:90%
I don't get it.

01:12:57.132 --> 01:13:00.865 align:middle line:84%
If you explain the tenth time,
maybe-- maybe it was a cool

01:13:00.865 --> 01:13:03.540 align:middle line:84%
idea back home or with your
friend, maybe, but it just

01:13:03.540 --> 01:13:04.680 align:middle line:90%
doesn't work.

01:13:04.680 --> 01:13:07.900 align:middle line:84%
And so this experience
in the prototypes was

01:13:07.900 --> 01:13:09.310 align:middle line:90%
very healthy for us.

01:13:09.310 --> 01:13:10.580 align:middle line:90%
And then coming to the point.

01:13:10.580 --> 01:13:13.614 align:middle line:84%
And I realized for me it was
important that somebody coming

01:13:13.614 --> 01:13:16.230 align:middle line:84%
in the room, playing a prototype
for five minutes,

01:13:16.230 --> 01:13:18.880 align:middle line:84%
kind of understands
the core mechanic.

01:13:18.880 --> 01:13:21.130 align:middle line:90%
If this is not happening--

01:13:21.130 --> 01:13:24.416 align:middle line:84%
who takes the five minutes to
understand a core mechanic?

01:13:24.416 --> 01:13:25.620 align:middle line:90%
Nobody, today.

01:13:25.620 --> 01:13:27.000 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: That would be, like
in this game, just walking

01:13:27.000 --> 01:13:28.176 align:middle line:90%
onto a [INAUDIBLE].

01:13:28.176 --> 01:13:29.112 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Right.

01:13:29.112 --> 01:13:30.520 align:middle line:90%
That's right.

01:13:30.520 --> 01:13:32.800 align:middle line:90%
And so, yeah.

01:13:32.800 --> 01:13:35.350 align:middle line:84%
I think, coming from a different
perspective, again,

01:13:35.350 --> 01:13:40.030 align:middle line:84%
prototyping is kind of like the
way to get in the game.

01:13:40.030 --> 01:13:42.690 align:middle line:90%
Even as the designer.

01:13:42.690 --> 01:13:44.770 align:middle line:84%
Because from then on, you know
what you're talking about.

01:13:44.770 --> 01:13:46.682 align:middle line:84%
And so I had to lose a
lot of my abstract

01:13:46.682 --> 01:13:48.158 align:middle line:90%
thoughts in the process.

01:13:48.158 --> 01:13:49.470 align:middle line:90%
And then the playtesting.

01:13:49.470 --> 01:13:52.995 align:middle line:84%
So it was interesting sometimes
to see, even if the

01:13:52.995 --> 01:13:55.420 align:middle line:84%
prototype is cool, then
you have a prototype.

01:13:55.420 --> 01:13:57.610 align:middle line:84%
And then people come in and
say, [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:13:57.610 --> 01:13:58.480 align:middle line:90%
again.

01:13:58.480 --> 01:14:00.332 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:14:00.332 --> 01:14:01.260 align:middle line:90%
it's so easy.

01:14:01.260 --> 01:14:04.470 align:middle line:84%
But then you can start to stick
with your idea or say

01:14:04.470 --> 01:14:08.370 align:middle line:84%
no, maybe I have to go down with
the theoretical aspect

01:14:08.370 --> 01:14:10.780 align:middle line:90%
and really look [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:14:10.780 --> 01:14:12.030 align:middle line:90%
for the players.

01:14:12.030 --> 01:14:13.890 align:middle line:90%


01:14:13.890 --> 01:14:15.390 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And another thing
about prototyping, it's

01:14:15.390 --> 01:14:17.440 align:middle line:90%
not focus groups.

01:14:17.440 --> 01:14:21.540 align:middle line:84%
So it's not a case of, --sure
you want to put it in front of

01:14:21.540 --> 01:14:23.060 align:middle line:90%
as many people as possible.

01:14:23.060 --> 01:14:25.980 align:middle line:84%
But you really want to, I think,
avoid stuff like what

01:14:25.980 --> 01:14:27.852 align:middle line:84%
did you like, what
didn't you like.

01:14:27.852 --> 01:14:28.710 align:middle line:90%
Those kind of questions.

01:14:28.710 --> 01:14:30.110 align:middle line:84%
Because people want
to be helpful.

01:14:30.110 --> 01:14:34.140 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes the piece they
didn't like was actually the

01:14:34.140 --> 01:14:35.500 align:middle line:84%
part where they were
working hardest.

01:14:35.500 --> 01:14:36.810 align:middle line:84%
So it's like I didn't
like that I had to

01:14:36.810 --> 01:14:37.800 align:middle line:90%
jump over the wall.

01:14:37.800 --> 01:14:40.780 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] jump
over the wall.

01:14:40.780 --> 01:14:43.140 align:middle line:84%
And it may not really mean that
if you took that out,

01:14:43.140 --> 01:14:44.210 align:middle line:84%
that they would enjoy
the game more.

01:14:44.210 --> 01:14:45.710 align:middle line:84%
In fact, they might not
enjoy it at all.

01:14:45.710 --> 01:14:47.470 align:middle line:84%
So you have to be really
careful about that.

01:14:47.470 --> 01:14:49.004 align:middle line:90%
And people want to please you.

01:14:49.004 --> 01:14:49.960 align:middle line:84%
So they'll say oh, it's good,
and stuff like that.

01:14:49.960 --> 01:14:52.690 align:middle line:84%
I think it's really important
that what you do with

01:14:52.690 --> 01:14:56.236 align:middle line:84%
prototyping is watch
the player.

01:14:56.236 --> 01:14:59.110 align:middle line:84%
And then trust yourself
as a designer.

01:14:59.110 --> 01:15:02.160 align:middle line:84%
Rather than ask them what you
should do, because they will

01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:03.310 align:middle line:84%
try and be helpful and they'll
come up with wrong

01:15:03.310 --> 01:15:04.640 align:middle line:90%
suggestions.

01:15:04.640 --> 01:15:08.160 align:middle line:84%
Watch them and use your best
judgment as designer as to

01:15:08.160 --> 01:15:10.000 align:middle line:84%
what they're reacting to,
and why they're reacting

01:15:10.000 --> 01:15:11.360 align:middle line:90%
the way they are.

01:15:11.360 --> 01:15:13.350 align:middle line:90%
And try to fix it.

01:15:13.350 --> 01:15:16.730 align:middle line:84%
And then you try to get some
questions, try to maybe get

01:15:16.730 --> 01:15:17.820 align:middle line:90%
some access to their thinking.

01:15:17.820 --> 01:15:20.700 align:middle line:84%
But don't think that they
necessarily know what was

01:15:20.700 --> 01:15:25.150 align:middle line:84%
interesting to them, or what
would make them happy.

01:15:25.150 --> 01:15:26.060 align:middle line:90%
Don't ask those questions.

01:15:26.060 --> 01:15:29.006 align:middle line:84%
Those are focus group questions,
and you shouldn't

01:15:29.006 --> 01:15:30.601 align:middle line:84%
ask them because it's not
going to give you

01:15:30.601 --> 01:15:31.461 align:middle line:90%
the answers you want.

01:15:31.461 --> 01:15:33.425 align:middle line:84%
You've got to trust yourself
at some level

01:15:33.425 --> 01:15:34.420 align:middle line:90%
as to what's happening.

01:15:34.420 --> 01:15:36.570 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And something
that I also had to learn was,

01:15:36.570 --> 01:15:41.120 align:middle line:84%
if you have the focus test or
the playtest, and then you

01:15:41.120 --> 01:15:42.490 align:middle line:90%
realize you have problems.

01:15:42.490 --> 01:15:45.240 align:middle line:84%
And it's really healthy to
write all the problems.

01:15:45.240 --> 01:15:47.670 align:middle line:84%
And then brainstorm different
solutions.

01:15:47.670 --> 01:15:50.520 align:middle line:84%
Don't ever, when you discuss
it, try to come up with a

01:15:50.520 --> 01:15:51.510 align:middle line:90%
solution immediately.

01:15:51.510 --> 01:15:55.000 align:middle line:84%
Because what happens is, you
quickly develop one solution.

01:15:55.000 --> 01:15:56.940 align:middle line:84%
And then you go and end up with
another problem, but you

01:15:56.940 --> 01:16:00.870 align:middle line:84%
don't realize that sometimes,
some problems stick together.

01:16:00.870 --> 01:16:02.840 align:middle line:84%
Or sometimes you realize,
well, we have so many

01:16:02.840 --> 01:16:06.192 align:middle line:84%
problems, just because of this
little aspect of the game.

01:16:06.192 --> 01:16:07.600 align:middle line:84%
Why don't we just let
it [UNINTELLIGIBLE].

01:16:07.600 --> 01:16:10.305 align:middle line:84%
If you don't do that, you start
to spend two hours in

01:16:10.305 --> 01:16:13.430 align:middle line:84%
solving a problem then
realize it was wrong.

01:16:13.430 --> 01:16:14.900 align:middle line:90%
Or you never realize it again.

01:16:14.900 --> 01:16:15.910 align:middle line:90%
So have [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:16:15.910 --> 01:16:18.475 align:middle line:84%
problems and quick solutions
where you [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:16:18.475 --> 01:16:19.560 align:middle line:90%
or not.

01:16:19.560 --> 01:16:22.706 align:middle line:84%
Make solution, solution,
solution and then with the

01:16:22.706 --> 01:16:26.010 align:middle line:84%
same problem a few weeks
later again.

01:16:26.010 --> 01:16:28.680 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And I just want
to clarify, focus tests and

01:16:28.680 --> 01:16:30.740 align:middle line:84%
focus groups are two very
different things.

01:16:30.740 --> 01:16:33.360 align:middle line:90%


01:16:33.360 --> 01:16:35.560 align:middle line:84%
The kind of resource we're
talking about is focus

01:16:35.560 --> 01:16:38.040 align:middle line:84%
testing, which is specifically
identifying individual

01:16:38.040 --> 01:16:40.200 align:middle line:90%
problems in the game.

01:16:40.200 --> 01:16:43.755 align:middle line:84%
And a focus group is asking them
what they liked, which is

01:16:43.755 --> 01:16:46.980 align:middle line:84%
actually very not useful for
most product design in fact.

01:16:46.980 --> 01:16:49.230 align:middle line:90%
Games in particular.

01:16:49.230 --> 01:16:51.420 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: One piece of
advice that [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:16:51.420 --> 01:16:54.154 align:middle line:84%
really important if you're a
smart college student, like an

01:16:54.154 --> 01:16:57.802 align:middle line:84%
MIT student, is that you've
probably learned in spite of

01:16:57.802 --> 01:17:00.100 align:middle line:84%
your education, by having
someone sit in front of a

01:17:00.100 --> 01:17:02.000 align:middle line:84%
class and tell you what
was going on.

01:17:02.000 --> 01:17:03.860 align:middle line:90%
And you somehow absorbed it.

01:17:03.860 --> 01:17:06.650 align:middle line:84%
And that's why you've
got here.

01:17:06.650 --> 01:17:09.150 align:middle line:84%
But lots of people
don't learn that.

01:17:09.150 --> 01:17:12.616 align:middle line:84%
And it's really hard to avoid
the tendency in the game to

01:17:12.616 --> 01:17:14.340 align:middle line:84%
just tell people what
they should know.

01:17:14.340 --> 01:17:16.770 align:middle line:84%
Which is, sort of for my money,
like when you meet

01:17:16.770 --> 01:17:18.490 align:middle line:84%
someone who speaks a
foreign language.

01:17:18.490 --> 01:17:20.755 align:middle line:84%
You don't understand each
other so you both start

01:17:20.755 --> 01:17:24.450 align:middle line:90%
speaking slower and louder.

01:17:24.450 --> 01:17:25.450 align:middle line:84%
And I think that's what
a lot of education--

01:17:25.450 --> 01:17:27.850 align:middle line:84%
it's all a bad education
in general does.

01:17:27.850 --> 01:17:29.540 align:middle line:90%
Speaks slower and louder.

01:17:29.540 --> 01:17:30.300 align:middle line:90%
And [UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:17:30.300 --> 01:17:32.755 align:middle line:90%
educational games.

01:17:32.755 --> 01:17:34.360 align:middle line:84%
So just remember that just
because you got it the first

01:17:34.360 --> 01:17:37.210 align:middle line:84%
time someone told you doesn't
mean that everybody else--

01:17:37.210 --> 01:17:39.534 align:middle line:84%
it's not just about explaining
it better.

01:17:39.534 --> 01:17:43.020 align:middle line:84%
It's about giving them a
different way of learning.

01:17:43.020 --> 01:17:45.510 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Any questions
for our guests?

01:17:45.510 --> 01:17:55.970 align:middle line:90%


01:17:55.970 --> 01:17:56.670 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I just want
to say something.

01:17:56.670 --> 01:17:57.420 align:middle line:84%
Have you ever heard
of firehose games

01:17:57.420 --> 01:17:58.532 align:middle line:90%
[UNINTELLIGIBLE]

01:17:58.532 --> 01:17:59.210 align:middle line:90%
forum.

01:17:59.210 --> 01:18:02.010 align:middle line:84%
So I told them, I know a
guy who works there.

01:18:02.010 --> 01:18:05.250 align:middle line:84%
And he said, we've got a focus
group tomorrow night.

01:18:05.250 --> 01:18:07.655 align:middle line:84%
They're PS3 set to release
in a few months.

01:18:07.655 --> 01:18:09.840 align:middle line:84%
Does anyone want to
go tomorrow night?

01:18:09.840 --> 01:18:12.280 align:middle line:84%
Seven o'clock, about an hour
to an hour and a half.

